{{2025 Australian federal election sidebar}}
The '''2025 Australian federal election''' was held on Saturday, 3 May 2025, to elect members of the [[48th Parliament of Australia]]. Up for election were all 150 seats in the [[Australian House of Representatives|House of Representatives]] and 40 of the 76 seats in the [[Australian Senate|Senate]]. The incumbent [[Albanese government|Labor government]], led by [[Anthony Albanese]], won re-election to a second consecutive term in office in a [[landslide victory]] over the [[Opposition (Australia)|Oppostion]] [[Coalition (Australia)|Liberal-National Coalition]], led by [[Peter Dutton]].<ref name="The Guardian live" /><ref name="Worthington 2025" /><ref name="Olbrycht-Palmer & Williamson 2025" /> The result was generally considered an [[Upset (competition)|upset]], with most [[opinion polling]] having underestimated the size of [[Australian Labor Party|Labor]]'s victory. Labor won the highest number of seats in the House of Representatives in the party's history, winning 93 seats (as of 12 May 2025).

It was the fourth time in history that a [[Australian government|government]] secured 90 or more seats in a federal election—the last such instance being in [[2013 Australian federal election|2013]]. This was the first time a single party, and specifically the Labor Party, achieved this feat and this was also the second largest number of seats won by a party or coalition in Australian electoral history, greater than the [[1975 Australian federal election|1975]] election result and just short of the [[1996 Australian federal election|1996]] election result's 94 seats (with two seats still left to be called). Labor also lost no seats to any other party, making them the first incumbent party of government to not lose any seats since [[Harold Holt]]'s Coalition in the [[1966 Australian federal election|1966 election]].

Labor grew its parliamentary majority by gaining seats from incumbents on both flanks of the [[political spectrum]]. Meanwhile, the Coalition suffered a large swing against them, particularly in urban areas. The [[Liberal Party of Australia|Liberal Party]], the Coalition's senior party, suffered its worst federal result in terms of voteshare and seats since its formation in 1944. Liberal leader Peter Dutton lost his own seat of [[Division of Dickson|Dickson]] to Labor candidate [[Ali France]], the first time an Opposition Leader had been defeated in their own seat. The [[Australian Greens|Greens]] also had a swing against them, losing three of their four seats in the House of Representatives, including that of their leader [[Adam Bandt]], who lost his seat of [[Division of Melbourne|Melbourne]] to Labor.

The [[Australian Broadcasting Corporation]] (ABC) projected a second term for Labor within two and a half hours of [[Eastern states of Australia|east coast]] polls closing. Dutton conceded defeat shortly after 9:30{{nbsp}}pm [[Time in Australia|AEST]] on election night, announcing that he had called Albanese to congratulate him on Labor's re-election.<ref name="Messenger 2025" /><ref name="Reuters Dutton" /><ref name="Landslide" />  The pertinent issues throughout the campaign were the cost of living, energy policy, housing, healthcare and defence.<ref name="Chrysanthos 2025" /> The Coalition campaigned on building seven [[nuclear power plants in Australia]] over 20 years, while Labor promised to build 1.2 million new homes. Both parties supported an increase in defence spending. The influence of US President [[Donald Trump]] was also attributed as a major factor in the results, with several outlets ascribing Labor's victory to anti-Trump sentiment.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/count-under-way-australia-election-with-living-costs-trump-focus-2025-05-03/|title=Australia's Albanese claims election victory, riding anti-Trump wave|work=[[Reuters]]|date=3 May 2025|accessdate=3 May 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/2025/05/03/australia-labor-albanese-trump-election/|title=Australia's Labor Party, buoyed by anti-Trump bump, wins reelection|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|first=Michael E.|last=Miller|date=3 May 2025|accessdate=3 May 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.politico.eu/article/donald-trump-cost-conservative-australia-election-takeaway/|title=How Trump lost conservatives the Australian election|work=[[Politico]]|date=3 May 2025|accessdate=16 May 2025}}</ref>

==Background==
===Previous election===
{{main|2022 Australian federal election}}
{{see also|Results of the 2022 Australian federal election (House of Representatives)|Results of the 2022 Australian federal election (Senate)}}
At the previous election in May 2022, the [[Australian Labor Party|Labor Party]], led by [[Anthony Albanese]], formed a government after nine years in opposition, winning 77 seats in the House of Representatives, enough for a two-seat majority. The [[Liberal Party of Australia|Liberal]]–[[National Party of Australia|National]] [[Coalition (Australia)|Coalition]] that had previously governed won only 58 seats and went into [[Opposition (Australia)|opposition]]. The [[Crossbencher|crossbench]], made up of other parties and [[Independent politician|independents]], expanded to 16 seats: ten held by independents (including seven held by a loose grouping of [[teal independents]]), four by the [[Australian Greens|Greens]], and one each by the  [[Centre Alliance]] and [[Katter's Australian Party]].<ref>{{cite news|first=Antony|last=Green|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/elections/federal/2022/results|date=23 June 2022|orig-date=21 March 2022|title=Australian Federal Election 2022 Live Results|website=ABC News|access-date=2 June 2022|archive-date=21 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220521011310/https://www.abc.net.au/news/elections/federal/2022/results|url-status=live}}</ref>

In the Senate, Labor made no gains and remained steady at 26 seats overall, thus requiring 13 additional votes in the Senate to pass legislation. The Coalition lost four seats and retained only 32 seats. The Greens gained three seats to 12. [[Pauline Hanson's One Nation|One Nation]] also remained steady with two seats, [[Centre Alliance]] and [[Rex Patrick Team]] each lost their Senate seats, while the [[Jacqui Lambie Network]] gained a second seat. [[David Pocock]] was elected as an independent senator on his [[David Pocock (political party)|own ticket]], and the [[United Australia Party (2013)|United Australia Party]] also gained one seat.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/elections/federal/2022/results/senate|date=23 June 2022|orig-date=21 March 2022|title=Senate Results|website=ABC News|access-date=22 June 2022|archive-date=13 November 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231113044217/https://www.abc.net.au/news/elections/federal/2022/results/senate|url-status=live|first=Antony|last=Green|author-link=Antony Green}}</ref>

===Composition of Parliament===
{{see also|47th Parliament of Australia#Changes in membership}}
The [[47th Parliament of Australia|47th Parliament]] opened on 26 July 2022. The Liberal Party entered the parliament with a new leader, with former defence and home affairs minister [[Peter Dutton]] replacing the outgoing prime minister [[Scott Morrison]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-05-30/peter-dutton-liberal-party-leader-sussan-ley-deputy/101109498|title=Peter Dutton elected new Liberal Party leader, Sussan Ley becomes deputy leader|date=30 May 2022|website=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]|last=Hitch |first=Georgia|access-date=30 May 2022|archive-date=30 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220530171734/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-05-30/peter-dutton-liberal-party-leader-sussan-ley-deputy/101109498|url-status=live}}</ref> On 23 December 2022, [[National Party of Australia|Nationals]] MP for [[Division of Calare|Calare]], [[Andrew Gee (politician)|Andrew Gee]], left the party and became an [[Independent politician|independent]], following the party's decision to publicly oppose an [[Indigenous Voice to Parliament]]. This change of parties caused the Crossbench to increase to 17 seats,  with the Coalition decreasing to 57 seats.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/dec/23/nationals-mp-andrew-gee-quits-party-citing-opposition-to-indigenous-voice|title=Nationals MP Andrew Gee quits party citing its opposition to Indigenous voice|date=23 December 2022|website=[[Guardian Australia|The Guardian]]|first=Paul |last=Karp|access-date=23 December 2022|archive-date=4 April 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404083907/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/dec/23/nationals-mp-andrew-gee-quits-party-citing-opposition-to-indigenous-voice|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 16 January 2023, Liberal senator [[Jim Molan]] died and was replaced by [[Maria Kovacic]] in May that year. On 6 February 2023, [[Australian Greens|Greens]] senator [[Lidia Thorpe]] resigned from the party to sit as an independent. On 1 April 2023, Labor's [[Mary Doyle]] won the [[2023 Aston by-election]] following the resignation of sitting Liberal MP [[Alan Tudge]]. The result was considered a major [[Upset (competition)|upset]] and marked the first time that an incumbent government had won a seat from the Opposition since the [[1920 Kalgoorlie by-election]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2023-04-01/byelection-result-aston-melbourne-labor-win/102157990|first=Andi|last=Yu|date=1 April 2023|title=Labor snatches historic victory in Aston by-election in Melbourne's outer east|website=ABC News|access-date=1 April 2023|archive-date=13 April 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230413063618/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2023-04-01/byelection-result-aston-melbourne-labor-win/102157990|url-status=live}}</ref> As a result, Labor increased their number of seats in the House of Representatives to 78, while the Coalition decreased to 56. In May 2023, incumbent [[Liberal National Party of Queensland|Liberal National]] MP [[Stuart Robert]] resigned, triggering [[2023 Fadden by-election|another by-election]], this time in the seat of [[Division of Fadden|Fadden]] on the [[Gold Coast, Queensland|Gold Coast]]. The seat was won by Liberal National candidate [[Cameron Caldwell]], keeping the composition of the parliament unchanged. Also in May 2023, [[Dai Le]], the independent member for the seat of [[Division of Fowler|Fowler]] in [[Greater Western Sydney|Western Sydney]], formed her own political party, the [[Dai Le and Frank Carbone Network]], alongside [[Frank Carbone (politician)|Frank Carbone]], the [[City of Fairfield#Mayor|Mayor of Fairfield]]. The party would be primarily based in Western Sydney.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/may/14/independent-mp-dai-le-looks-to-spin-success-in-western-sydney-into-new-political-movement |work=The Guardian | title=Independent MP Dai le looks to spin success in western Sydney into new political movement | access-date=18 May 2023 | archive-date=18 May 2023 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230518045253/https://theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/may/14/independent-mp-dai-le-looks-to-spin-success-in-western-sydney-into-new-political-movement |name-list-style=and |first1=Mostafa |last1=Rachwani |last2=Rose |first2=Tamsin |date=14 May 2023 | url-status=live}}</ref>

On 15 June 2023, Liberal senator [[David Van]] was expelled from the party following sexual misconduct allegations by former LNP senator [[Amanda Stoker]] and independent senator [[Lidia Thorpe]]. He continued his term as an independent. On 14 November 2023, following a party preselection defeat, Liberal MP [[Russell Broadbent]] left the party to sit on the crossbench.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2023-11-14/russell-broadbent-quits-liberal-party-after-preselection-loss/103102336 |title=Outspoken backbencher Russell Broadbent quits Liberal Party after preselection loss |first1=Millicent |last1=Spencer |last2=Whittaker |first2=Jarrod |newspaper=ABC News |date=14 November 2023 |access-date=14 November 2023 |archive-date=14 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231114020720/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2023-11-14/russell-broadbent-quits-liberal-party-after-preselection-loss/103102336 |url-status=live}}</ref> November also saw [[Dave Sharma]] return to parliament, this time as a Liberal Senator, after the resignation of party veteran [[Marise Payne]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=2023-09-08 |title=Liberal senator Marise Payne to retire from politics |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2023-09-08/liberal-senator-marise-payne-to-retire-from-politics/102165366 |first=Jake |last=Evans |access-date=2024-10-17 |work=ABC News |language=en-AU |archive-date=7 October 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241007184834/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2023-09-08/liberal-senator-marise-payne-to-retire-from-politics/102165366 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Remeikis |first=Amy |date=2023-11-26 |title=Moderate Dave Sharma wins NSW Liberal Senate spot after beating both Peter Dutton-endorsed candidates |url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/nov/26/moderate-dave-sharma-wins-nsw-liberal-senate-spot-after-beating-both-peter-dutton-endorsed-candidates |access-date=2024-10-17 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077 |archive-date=26 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231126071151/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/nov/26/moderate-dave-sharma-wins-nsw-liberal-senate-spot-after-beating-both-peter-dutton-endorsed-candidates |url-status=live }}</ref> On 4 December 2023, Labor MP [[Peta Murphy]] died of cancer, reducing Labor to 77 seats, though the party's share was restored to 78 seats on 2 March 2024, when candidate [[Jodie Belyea]] retained the seat of [[Division of Dunkley|Dunkley]] at the [[2024 Dunkley by-election|by-election]]. Similarly the Liberal Party fell by one seat on 28 February 2024, when Morrison (the former prime minister and MP) resigned from the seat of [[Division of Cook|Cook]], though Liberal candidate [[Simon Kennedy]] retained the seat for the party at the [[2024 Cook by-election]].

Labor senator [[Pat Dodson]] resigned from the Senate in January 2024 while undergoing cancer treatment.  His vacancy was filled by [[Varun Ghosh]]. Labor senator [[Linda White (politician)|Linda White]] died in March 2024 and was replaced by [[Lisa Darmanin (politician)|Lisa Darmanin]], while Greens senator [[Janet Rice]] resigned the following month and was replaced by [[Steph Hodgins-May]]. Party-compositional changes occurred when Tasmanian senator [[Tammy Tyrrell]] quit the [[Jacqui Lambie Network]] to sit as an independent on 28 March 2024 and Labor senator [[Fatima Payman]] left the party and joined the crossbench as an independent in July 2024, citing disagreement with the party's position concerning the [[Israeli invasion of the Gaza Strip|Israel–Gaza conflict]]. Three months later, Payman established the [[Australia's Voice]] party, stating that she intended for the party to field candidates in both houses of parliament at this election. The Senate composition changed once again on 25 August 2024 when LNP senator [[Gerard Rennick]] resigned from the party and moved to the crossbench to sit as an independent following a preselection defeat. Like Payman, he announced his intention to establish a political party, named the [[People First Party (Australia)|People First Party]], so that his name would be featured above the line on the election ballot.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/aug/26/gerard-rennick-quits-lnp-liberal-national-launch-people-first-party|author=Australian Associated Press|access-date=21 April 2025|title=Gerard Rennick quits LNP and reveals plan to register 'People First' party|work=The Guardian|date=26 August 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250328102414/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/aug/26/gerard-rennick-quits-lnp-liberal-national-launch-people-first-party|url-status=live|archive-date=28 March 2025}}</ref>

On 28 January 2025, Liberal senator [[Simon Birmingham]] resigned from Parliament. The following week, on 6 February 2025, [[Leah Blyth]] was appointed to the Senate as his replacement. Two lower house seats were left vacant prior to the election. Liberal National MP [[Keith Pitt]], who sat in the Nationals party room, resigned from the seat of [[Division of Hinkler|Hinkler]] on 19 January 2025, and the following day Labor MP [[Bill Shorten]] departed from the seat of [[Division of Maribyrnong|Maribyrnong]]. With their resignations occurring sufficiently close to the federal election, by-elections were not held.

===Events of the 47th Parliament===
{{See also|47th Parliament of Australia#Major events and legislation}}
Both major parties retained their leaders throughout the duration of the 47th Parliament, with Anthony Albanese having served one full term as Prime Minister and a second consecutive term as leader of the Labor Party, while Peter Dutton completed his first full term as Opposition Leader and leader of the Liberal Party. The [[First Albanese ministry|Albanese ministry]] was reshuffled in July 2024, followed by a minor reshuffle in January 2025, while the [[shadow ministry of Peter Dutton]] was reshuffled in April 2023, March 2024, and January 2025.

The [[2023 Australian Indigenous Voice referendum]] featured prominently in political discourse during the first half of the parliamentary term. First proposed in the 2017 ''[[Uluru Statement from the Heart]]'', an [[Indigenous Voice to Parliament]] was supported by the Labor Party as part of its 2022 election platform. The Yes campaign in support of the Voice initially attracted some bipartisan support, including Coalition figures such as Gee, former Minister for Indigenous Australians [[Ken Wyatt]], and then-Shadow Minister for Indigenous Australians [[Julian Leeser]];<ref>{{Cite news |last=Zimmerman |first=Trent |author-link=Trent Zimmerman |date=2023-04-12 |title=Indigenous voice opposition has cost the Liberals Julian Leeser and Ken Wyatt. The own goals are mounting |url=https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2023/apr/12/indigenous-voice-opposition-has-cost-the-liberals-julian-leeser-and-ken-wyatt-the-own-goals-are-mounting |url-status=live |archive-date=22 November 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241122005612/https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2023/apr/12/indigenous-voice-opposition-has-cost-the-liberals-julian-leeser-and-ken-wyatt-the-own-goals-are-mounting |access-date=2024-12-06 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> however, the National Party came out against the Voice in November 2022, as did the Liberal Party in April 2023. First-term Country Liberal Party Senator [[Jacinta Nampijinpa Price]], who succeeded Leeser as Shadow Minister for Indigenous Australians, took on a prominent role in the No campaign.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2024-05-03 |title=NT Senator Jacinta Nampijinpa Price on the Voice to Parliament referendum and speaking out as a conservative Aboriginal woman |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-05-03/jacinta-nampijinpa-price-voice-referendum-no-campaign-reflection/103779330|first=Eleni|last=Roussos |access-date=2024-12-06 |work=ABC News |language=en-AU |archive-date=22 November 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241122005613/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-05-03/jacinta-nampijinpa-price-voice-referendum-no-campaign-reflection/103779330 |url-status=live }}</ref> In August 2023, Albanese announced the referendum would be held on 14 October 2023.<ref>{{cite news |work=SBS News |url=https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/when-will-we-vote-on-the-voice-to-parliament-we-now-know/1we5trvbp |access-date=21 April 2025 |archive-date=9 October 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241009145358/https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/when-will-we-vote-on-the-voice-to-parliament-we-now-know/1we5trvbp|url-status=live|date=30 August 2023|first=Finn |last=McHugh |title=When will we vote on the Voice to Parliament? We now know}}</ref> 60% of voters, including a majority in all six states, voted against the proposed constitutional changes.<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2023-10-15/voice-to-parliament-referendum-result-no-vote-yes-campaign/102977654 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250218212622/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2023-10-15/voice-to-parliament-referendum-result-no-vote-yes-campaign/102977654 |archive-date=18 February 2025 |title=The Voice to Parliament referendum was defeated. Let's catch you up on the results |date=15 October 2023 |access-date=21 April 2025 |first=Danielle |last=Maguire}}</ref>

The [[death and state funeral of Elizabeth II]], the long-serving head of state of Australia and other Commonwealth realms, took place in September 2022, followed by the [[coronation of Charles III and Camilla]] in May 2023; as a result, 2025 was the first federal election under the reign of Charles III. King Charles [[2024 royal tour of Australia|visited Australia in October 2024]], the first visit by a ruling monarch since 2011. Independent Senator [[Lidia Thorpe]], who had resigned from the Greens in February 2023 over disagreements regarding the proposed Voice to Parliament, attracted significant media attention for shouting at Charles during an event at Parliament House in Canberra.<ref>{{Cite web |first1=Katy |last1=Watson |last2=Relph |first2=Daniela |location=Canberra |date=22 October 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250402210400/https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c79n20r750po |url-status=live |archive-date=2 April 2025 |name-list-style=and |title=Indigenous Australian senator defends heckling King |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c79n20r750po |access-date=2024-12-06 |website=BBC News |language=en-GB}}</ref> [[Israel–Hamas war protests in Australia]] began in October 2023; responses to the war, particularly among local [[Australian Jews|Jewish]] and [[Islam in Australia|Muslim]] communities, were perceived as a significant threat to peaceful discourse in Australia. Incidents of antisemitism and Islamophobia also increased, prompting the government to appoint three "special envoys": in July 2024, [[Jillian Segal]] as Special Envoy to Combat Antisemitism, and Labor MP [[Peter Khalil]] as Special Envoy for Social Cohesion; and in September 2024, Aftab Malik as Special Envoy to Combat Islamophobia.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-07-29 |title=Australia appoints social cohesion envoy to ease community tensions |url=https://www.voanews.com/a/australia-appoints-social-cohesion-envoy-to-ease-community-tensions-/7716795.html |first=Phil |last=Mercer |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250403031300/https://www.voanews.com/a/australia-appoints-social-cohesion-envoy-to-ease-community-tensions-/7716795.html |archive-date=3 April 2025 |access-date=2024-12-06 |website=[[Voice of America]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=2024-09-30 |title=UN 'Global Expert' Aftab Malik named Islamophobia envoy, after Imam Council warnings over delay |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-09-30/islamophobia-envoy-aftab-malik-appointed/104414330 |first=Jake |last=Evans|access-date=2024-12-06 |work=ABC News |language=en-AU |archive-date=1 February 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250201035736/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-09-30/islamophobia-envoy-aftab-malik-appointed/104414330 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Climate change remained a prominent issue, partly due to the impact of natural disasters, including the [[2022 south eastern Australia floods]] and the [[2023–24 Australian bushfire season]], which led to seven deaths and ten deaths respectively, as well as [[Cyclone Jasper]] and [[Cyclone Alfred (2025)|Cyclone Alfred]], which caused significant property damage on the eastern coast in December 2023 and March 2025, respectively. Changes to government infrastructure included: the establishment of the [[National Anti-Corruption Commission (Australia)|National Anti-Corruption Commission]] in July 2023; the launch of the [[Housing Australia Future Fund]] in November 2023;<ref>{{Cite news |date=2023-09-14 |url-status=live |archive-date=31 March 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250331071236/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2023-09-14/housing-australia-future-fund-passes-parliament/102844098 |title=Government's $10bn Housing Australia Future Fund passes parliament |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2023-09-14/housing-australia-future-fund-passes-parliament/102844098 |first=Stephanie |last=Borys |access-date=2024-12-06 |work=ABC News |language=en-AU}}</ref> the replacement of the [[Administrative Appeals Tribunal]] with the [[Administrative Review Tribunal]] in October 2024; and the passage of the ''[[Online Safety Amendment]]'' Bill in November 2024.<ref>{{cite news |work=[[Reuters]] |date=29 November 2024 |title=Australia passes social media ban for children under 16 |url=https://www.reuters.com/technology/australia-passes-social-media-ban-children-under-16-2024-11-28/ |access-date=6 April 2025 |first1=Byron |last1=Kaye |last2=Menon |first2=Praveen |first3=Supantha |last3=Mukherjee |last4=Chaven-Dreyfuss |first4=Gertrude |location=Sydney, Stockholm, New York |editor-first1=Alasdair |editor-last1=Pal |editor-last2=Paul |editor-first2=Sonali |editor-first3=Deepa |editor-last3=Babington |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20241129011231/https://www.reuters.com/technology/australia-passes-social-media-ban-children-under-16-2024-11-28/ |archive-date=29 November 2024}}</ref>

