==Electoral system==
Members of the House of Representatives are elected by full [[instant-runoff voting|preferential voting]]. Each [[Divisions of the Australian House of Representatives|electorate]] elects one member. Senators are elected by [[proportional representation]] using [[single transferable vote]]. In states, senators are elected from state-wide twelve-member districts (although in most cases only six seats are contested at a single election), and in territories from territory-wide two-member districts. Ballots are counted at least twice, at the polling place and, starting Monday night after election day, at counting centres.<ref name="aec-house">{{Cite web |title=House of Representatives count |url=https://aec.gov.au/Voting/counting/hor.htm |access-date=2022-08-27 |publisher=Australian Electoral Commission |language=en-AU |archive-date=16 January 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250116113024/https://aec.gov.au/Voting/counting/hor.htm |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="aec-sen">{{Cite web |title=The Senate counting process |url=https://aec.gov.au/Voting/counting/senate.htm |access-date=2022-08-27 |publisher=Australian Electoral Commission |language=en-AU |archive-date=21 January 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250121012822/https://aec.gov.au/Voting/counting/senate.htm |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Redistribution===
{{see also|Divisions of the Australian House of Representatives|Redistribution (Australia)}}
The [[Australian Electoral Commission]] (AEC) is required, one year after the first sitting day for a new House of Representatives, to determine the number of members to which each state and territory is entitled. If the number in any state changes, a redistribution would be required in those states. A redistribution would be postponed if it would begin within one year of the expiration of the House of Representatives. The apportionment determination was made in July 2023 based on the population figures for December 2022. The determination resulted in a reduction of one seat in New South Wales to 46, a reduction of one seat in Victoria to 38 and an increase of one seat in Western Australia to 16. The total number of seats in the House of Representatives would decrease from 151 to 150 at the 2025 federal election.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.aec.gov.au/media/2023/07-27.htm|title=House of Representatives to return to 150 members|publisher=Australian Electoral Commission|date=27 July 2023|access-date=18 October 2023|archive-date=20 October 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231020115418/https://www.aec.gov.au/media/2023/07-27.htm|url-status=live}}</ref>

In May and June 2024, the AEC released its draft proposals for electorate changes, recommending the creation of the [[Division of Bullwinkel]] (Western Australia) in the outer eastern suburbs of [[Perth]], and the abolition of the [[Division of Higgins]] (Victoria) in Melbourne's inner south-east (held by Labor's [[Michelle Ananda-Rajah]]) and the [[Division of North Sydney]] (New South Wales) in Sydney's inner north-east (held by [[Kylea Tink]], a [[teal independent]]).<ref>{{cite web|url=https://theconversation.com/the-aec-has-redrawn-the-boundaries-for-federal-seats-how-will-this-affect-the-next-election-232602|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240912042216/https://theconversation.com/the-aec-has-redrawn-the-boundaries-for-federal-seats-how-will-this-affect-the-next-election-232602|archive-date=12 September 2024|title=The AEC has redrawn the boundaries for federal seats. How will this affect the next election?|work=The Conversation|date=19 June 2024|first=Adrian |last=Beaumont}}</ref> In addition, the commission proposed altering the boundaries of several seats in all three states.

The Western Australia and Victoria-based changes were confirmed by the commission on 5 September 2024,<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.aec.gov.au/media/2024/09-05a.htm|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250404132006/https://www.aec.gov.au/media/2024/09-05a.htm|archive-date=4 April 2025|title=Names and boundaries of federal electoral divisions in Western Australia decided|publisher=Australian Electoral Commission|date=5 September 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite press release|access-date=21 April 2025|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250328202923/https://www.aec.gov.au/media/2024/09-05b.htm|archive-date=28 March 2025|url=https://www.aec.gov.au/media/2024/09-05b.htm|title=Names and boundaries of federal electoral divisions in Victoria decided|publisher=Australian Electoral Commission|date=5 September 2024}}</ref> with the new boundaries gazetted respectively on 24 September and 17 October.<ref>{{cite web|archive-url=https://archive.today/20250421052120/https://www.legislation.gov.au/C2024G00582/asmade/text|id=C2024G00582|url-status=live|archive-date=21 April 2025 |url=https://www.legislation.gov.au/C2024G00582/asmade/text|title=Determination of names and boundaries of federal electoral divisions in Western Australia|via=Federal Register of Legislation|date=24 September 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.legislation.gov.au/C2024G00617/asmade/text|url-status=live|archive-date=21 April 2025|id=C2024G00617|archive-url=https://archive.today/20250421063103/https://www.legislation.gov.au/C2024G00617/asmade/text|title=Determination of names and boundaries of federal electoral divisions in Victoria|date=17 October 2024|via=Federal Register of Legislation}}</ref> The New South Wales changes were confirmed on 12 September,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.aec.gov.au/media/2024/09-12.htm|access-date=21 April 2025|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250323110345/https://www.aec.gov.au/media/2024/09-12.htm|archive-date=23 March 2025|title=Names and boundaries of federal electoral divisions in New South Wales decided|publisher=Australian Electoral Commission|date=12 September 2024}}</ref> and were gazetted on 10 October 2024.<ref>{{cite web|url-status=live|archive-url=https://archive.today/20250421051946/https://www.legislation.gov.au/C2024G00607/asmade/text|id=C2024G00607|url=https://www.legislation.gov.au/C2024G00607/asmade/text|title=Determination of names and boundaries of federal electoral divisions in New South Wales|via=legislation.gov.au|date=10 October 2024|access-date=15 October 2024|archive-date=21 April 2025}}</ref> According to [[Antony Green]], some of the more significant changes to existing electorates included: the [[Division of Hasluck]], losing much of its area to the new seat of Bullwinkel and shifting westwards into Perth's northeastern suburbs;<ref>{{Cite web |last=Green |first=Antony |date=2024-09-05 |title=2024 Federal Redistribution – Final Boundaries for Western Australia Released |url=https://antonygreen.com.au/2025-federal-redistribution-final-boundaries-for-western-australia-released/ |access-date=2024-12-06 |website=Antony Green's Election Blog |language=en-AU |archive-date=21 November 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241121214943/https://antonygreen.com.au/2025-federal-redistribution-final-boundaries-for-western-australia-released/ |url-status=live }}</ref> in Melbourne, the Divisions of [[Division of Melbourne|Melbourne]] and [[Division of Wills|Wills]] moving to the south, and the Divisions of [[Division of Chisholm|Chisholm]] and [[Division of Menzies|Menzies]] moving westwards;<ref>{{Cite web |last=Green |first=Antony |date=2024-09-05 |title=2024 Federal Redistributions – Final Boundaries for Victoria Released |url=https://antonygreen.com.au/2024-federal-redistributions-final-boundaries-for-victoria-released/ |access-date=2024-12-06 |website=Antony Green's Election Blog |language=en-AU |archive-date=3 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241203014856/https://antonygreen.com.au/2024-federal-redistributions-final-boundaries-for-victoria-released/ |url-status=live }}</ref> the [[Division of Riverina]] shifting south-east and losing the towns of [[West Wyalong]], [[Parkes, New South Wales|Parkes]] and [[Forbes, New South Wales|Forbes]]; and the [[Division of Hume]] losing the majority of its area in the south, including the city of [[Goulburn]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://antonygreen.com.au/2024-federal-redistributions-final-boundaries-for-nsw-released/ |access-date=6 April 2025 |date=12 September 2024 |first=Antony |last=Green |work=Antony Green's Election Blog |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250210021956/https://antonygreen.com.au/2024-federal-redistributions-final-boundaries-for-nsw-released/ |url-status=live |archive-date=10 February 2025 |title=2024 Federal Redistributions – Final Boundaries for NSW Released}}</ref> There was also a small adjustment to the [[Northern Territory]]'s two federal electorates, with the [[Division of Solomon]] gaining some of the eastern suburbs of [[Palmerston, Northern Territory|Palmerston]] from the [[Division of Lingiari]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Green |first=Antony |date=2024-10-18 |title=2024 Federal Redistributions – Draft Northern Territory Boundaries |url=https://antonygreen.com.au/2024-federal-redistributions-draft-northern-territory-boundaries/ |access-date=2024-12-06 |website=Antony Green's Election Blog |language=en-AU |archive-date=21 November 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241121214943/https://antonygreen.com.au/2024-federal-redistributions-draft-northern-territory-boundaries/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

A redistribution was due to take place for Tasmania's electoral boundaries in November 2024, due to it being seven years since the day of the last determination in the state; however, this was deferred as it would occur within one year of the expiration of the House of Representatives. The redistribution would instead commence within 30 days after the first sitting day of the new House of Representatives in the [[48th Parliament of Australia]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.aec.gov.au/redistributions/2024/tas/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250402223946/https://www.aec.gov.au/redistributions/2024/tas/|publisher=Augmented Electoral Commission for Tasmania|archive-date=2 April 2025|url-status=live|title=Tasmanian redistribution|via=Australian Electoral Commission|access-date=22 January 2025}}</ref>

===Voter registration===
Enrolment of eligible voters is compulsory. Voters must notify the AEC within 8 weeks of a change of address or after turning 18. The electoral rolls are closed for new enrolments or update of details about a week after the issue of [[Writ of election#Australia|writs for election]].<ref name=CEAs155>{{cite Legislation AU|Cth|act|cea1918233|Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918|155}}</ref> Enrolment is optional for 16 or 17-year-olds, but they cannot vote until they turn 18,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.aec.gov.au/enrol/|title=Enrol to vote|publisher=Australian Electoral Commission|access-date=23 April 2021|archive-date=1 October 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191001230634/https://www.aec.gov.au/enrol/|url-status=live}}</ref> and persons who have applied for Australian citizenship may also apply for provisional enrolment which takes effect on the granting of citizenship.<ref>{{cite Legislation AU|Cth|act|cea1918233|Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918|99A}}</ref>

==Election date==
{{see also|Electoral system of Australia#Election day}}

===Constitutional and legal provisions===
The constitutional and legal provisions which impact on the choice of election dates include:<ref>{{cite web|last = Lundie|first = Rob|date = 25 June 2009|url = http://www.aph.gov.au/library/pubs/BN/2008-09/aust_elections.htm|title = Australian elections timetable|publisher = [[Parliamentary Library of Australia]], [[Parliament of Australia]]|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20110108212031/http://www.aph.gov.au/library/pubs/bn/2008-09/Aust_elections.htm|archive-date = 8 January 2011|url-status = dead}}</ref><ref name=green_date>{{cite web|last1=Riga|first1=Jessica|archive-date=5 September 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240905024053/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-04-10/federal-election-live-may-21-scott-morrison/100960362|url-status=live|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-04-10/federal-election-live-may-21-scott-morrison/100960362/|title=Federal election: Scott Morrison says 'this election is about you', Anthony Albanese says he wants to 'restore faith' in politics|work=ABC News|date=10 April 2022|at=Prime Minister Scott Morrison announces May 21 election|access-date=9 April 2022}}</ref>
* [[Section 12 of the Constitution of Australia|Section 12 of the Constitution]] says: "The Governor of any State may cause writs to be issued for the election of Senators for that State."<ref name = CoAs12>{{cite Legislation AU|Cth|act|coaca430|Commonwealth of Australia Constitution Act|12}}</ref>
* [[Section 13 of the Constitution of Australia|Section 13 of the Constitution]] provides that the election of senators shall be held in the period of twelve months before the places become vacant.<ref name = CoAs13>{{cite Legislation AU|Cth|act|coaca430|Commonwealth of Australia Constitution Act|13}}</ref>
* [[Section 28 of the Constitution of Australia|Section 28 of the Constitution]] says: "Every House of Representatives shall continue for three years from the first sitting of the House, and no longer, but may be sooner dissolved by the Governor-General."<ref name=CoAs28>{{cite Legislation AU|Cth|act|coaca430|Commonwealth of Australia Constitution Act|28}}</ref> Since the 47th Parliament of Australia opened on 26 July 2022, it would expire on 25 July 2025.
* [[Section 32 of the Constitution of Australia|Section 32 of the Constitution]] says: "The writs shall be issued within ten days from the expiry of a House of Representatives or from the proclamation of a dissolution thereof."<ref name=CoAs32>{{cite Legislation AU|Cth|act|coaca430|Commonwealth of Australia Constitution Act|32}}</ref> Ten days after 25 July 2025 is 4 August 2025.
* Section 156(1) of the [[Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918]] (CEA) says: "The date fixed for the nomination of the candidates shall not be less than 10 days nor more than 27 days after the date of the writ."<ref name=CEAs156>{{cite Legislation AU|Cth|act|cea1918233|Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918|156}}</ref> Twenty-seven days after 4 August 2025 is 31 August 2025.
* Section 157 of the CEA says: "The date fixed for the polling shall not be less than 23 days nor more than 31 days after the date of nomination."<ref name=CEAs157>{{cite Legislation AU|Cth|act|cea1918233|Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918|157}}</ref> Thirty-one days after 31 August 2025 is 1 October 2025, a Wednesday.
* Section 158 of the CEA says: "The day fixed for the polling shall be a Saturday."<ref name=CEAs158>{{cite Legislation AU|Cth|act|cea1918233|Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918|158}}</ref> The Saturday before 1 October 2025 is 27 September 2025, which was the latest possible date for the lower house election.

The election of senators must take place within one year before the terms expire for half-Senate elections,<ref name = CoAs13/> so that the writs for a half-Senate election could not be issued earlier than 1 July 2024. Since campaigns are for a minimum of 33 days, the earliest ''possible'' date for a simultaneous House/half-Senate election was Saturday, 3 August 2024.<ref name=Timetable>{{cite web|title = 'So when is the next election?': Australian elections timetable as at January 2020|date = 13 January 2020|url = https://www.aph.gov.au/About_Parliament/Parliamentary_Departments/Parliamentary_Library/pubs/rp/rp1920/NextElection|first1 = Rob|last1 = Lundie|first2 = Laura|last2 = Schatz|first3 = Damon|last3 = Muller|publisher = [[Parliamentary Library of Australia]], [[Parliament of Australia]]|access-date = 2 April 2021|archive-date = 29 March 2021|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210329034202/https://www.aph.gov.au/About_Parliament/Parliamentary_Departments/Parliamentary_Library/pubs/rp/rp1920/NextElection|url-status = dead}}</ref> The latest that a half-Senate election could be held must allow time for the votes to be counted and the writs to be returned before the newly elected senators take office on 1 July 2025. The previous election's writs were returned on 24 June 2022, 34 days after the 2022 federal election.<ref>{{cite press release|title=AEC returns writs for 2022 federal election|url=https://www.aec.gov.au/media/2022/06-24.htm|publisher=Australian Electoral Commission|date=24 June 2022|access-date=26 July 2022|archive-date=3 July 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220703023621/https://www.aec.gov.au/media/2022/06-24.htm|url-status=live}}</ref> Using this time frame, the last possible date for a half-Senate election to take place was Saturday 17 May 2025.<ref name="NewTimetable">{{cite web|last=Muller|first=Damon|title=So when is the next federal election? A quick guide|url=https://www.aph.gov.au/About_Parliament/Parliamentary_departments/Parliamentary_Library/Research/Quick_Guides/2022-23/WhenIsTheNextElection2022|publisher=Parliament of Australia|date=22 August 2022|access-date=27 Nov 2024|archive-date=5 October 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241005055943/https://www.aph.gov.au/About_Parliament/Parliamentary_departments/Parliamentary_Library/Research/Quick_Guides/2022-23/WhenIsTheNextElection2022|url-status=live}}</ref>

A [[double dissolution]] (a deadlock-breaking provision to dissolve both houses of parliament) cannot be called within six months before the date of the expiry of the House of Representatives.<ref>{{cite Legislation AU|Cth|act|coaca430|Commonwealth of Australia Constitution Act|57}}</ref> That means that any double dissolution of the 47th Parliament would have had to be granted by 24 January 2025. Allowing for the same stages indicated above, the last ''possible'' date for a double dissolution election would have been 29 March 2025.<ref name="NewTimetable" /> This can only occur if a bill that passes the House of Representatives is rejected by the Senate twice, at least three months apart.

===Choice of election date===
On 5 September 2024, during the announcement by [[Minister for the National Disability Insurance Scheme|NDIS]] and [[Minister for Government Services|government services]] minister [[Bill Shorten]] of his impending retirement from politics, Prime Minister [[Anthony Albanese]] suggested that he may schedule the election to be held at a date later than Shorten's departure from Parliament in February 2025, while noting that the election was due by May 2025.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/sep/05/australia-news-live-rba-reserve-bank-interest-rates-anthony-albanese-peter-dutton-jim-chalmers-labor-coalition?CMP=share_btn_url&page=with%3Ablock-66d908ee8f081743e33b5ac9#block-66d908ee8f081743e33b5ac9|name-list-style=and|at=Albanese says there may be a gap between Shorten retiring and new member being elected|work=[[Guardian Australia|The Guardian]]|date=5 September 2024|access-date=5 September 2024|title=Severe weather warnings for parts of NSW and Victoria – as it happened|archive-date=5 September 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240905023315/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/sep/05/australia-news-live-rba-reserve-bank-interest-rates-anthony-albanese-peter-dutton-jim-chalmers-labor-coalition?CMP=share_btn_url&page=with%3Ablock-66d908ee8f081743e33b5ac9#block-66d908ee8f081743e33b5ac9|url-status=live|first1=Nino|last1=Bucci|first2=Emily|last2=Wind}}</ref> Consideration had to be given to the [[2025 Western Australian state election|Western Australian state election]] scheduled on 8 March 2025. Holding the federal election on that day would require the state election to be rescheduled to the following Saturday.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-11-13/wa-state-election-2025-possible-date-change/104592724|name-list-style=and|first1=Courtney|last1=Withers|last2=Shine|first2=Rhiannon|first3=Nicolas|last3=Perpitch|title=WA Premier Roger Cook examining possible contingency plans in case of federal election date clash|work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]|date=13 November 2024|access-date=7 March 2025|archive-date=7 March 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250307192211/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-11-13/wa-state-election-2025-possible-date-change/104592724|url-status=live}}</ref> Having the state and federal elections too close to each other was also not desirable; calling the election in early March for April would have required the [[2025 Australian federal budget]] scheduled for 25 March to be postponed to after the election. To prevent the campaigns for the state and federal elections from clashing, the federal election would ideally have to be called after the state election. Accounting for the minimum 33 day campaign period, the earliest possible date for the election would then have been 12 April 2025.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-11-12/federal-election-speculation-tasmania-candidates/104590156|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250330103648/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-11-12/federal-election-speculation-tasmania-candidates/104590156|archive-date=30 March 2025|name-list-style=and|first1=Jacob|last1=Greber|last2=Littler|first2=Ruby|work=ABC News|date=12 November 2024|access-date=12 November 2024|title=Early election speculation hits a fever pitch as Albanese announces Tasmania candidates, deflects WA clash}}</ref>

Concurrent with increasing media speculation in the first week of March 2025 that Albanese might call the federal election for 12 April,<ref>{{cite web |last1=Jeffrey |first1=Daniel |last2=Branco |first2=Jorge |date=5 March 2025 |title=There are only three possible federal election dates left. So when will it actually be held? |url=https://www.9news.com.au/national/federal-election-2025-when-is-it-earliest-latest-dates-double-dissolution-explained/a8dec667-4d33-46d6-b85a-ae4ea2b88b4b |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250307004924/https://www.9news.com.au/national/federal-election-2025-when-is-it-earliest-latest-dates-double-dissolution-explained/a8dec667-4d33-46d6-b85a-ae4ea2b88b4b |archive-date=7 March 2025 |access-date=7 March 2025 |work=[[9news.com.au|9News]]}}</ref> it was forecast that [[Cyclone Alfred (2025)|Cyclone Alfred]] would make landfall in and impact [[South East Queensland|south-east Queensland]] and northern New South Wales on or about 8 March (same date as the Western Australian state election). On 7 March, Albanese announced he had ruled out a 12 April election, and his government would deliver the budget on 25 March as scheduled. This was to prevent the need for Albanese to leave the recovery zone for Canberra to call the election, and to also prevent the announcement and start of an election campaign from clashing with dealing with the cyclone and recovery efforts.<ref>{{cite web |last=Norman |first=Jane |date=7 March 2025 |title=Anthony Albanese rules out April election, with federal budget set for later this month |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-03-07/federal-government-to-hand-down-budget/105024394 |access-date=7 March 2025 |work=ABC News}}</ref> With the following April Saturdays of 19 April and 26 April coinciding with significant nationwide [[long weekend]]s, i.e. the [[Easter]] and [[Anzac Day]] public holidays, holding an election on those days would likely be problematic and unpopular. This left the Saturdays of 3 May, 10 May or 17 May as the only plausible dates {{mdash}} of these, 3 May coincides with a long weekend in [[Queensland]] and the [[Northern Territory]] for [[Labour Day]] and [[May Day]], respectively.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Truu |first=Maani |date=6 February 2025 |title=It's a federal election year. So when will the election actually be held? |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-02-07/when-will-the-australian-federal-election-be/104871714 |access-date=6 May 2025 |work=ABC News}}</ref>

Ahead of the 2025 federal budget, there was speculation that Albanese would call the election either on the Friday or Sunday following the budget, with potential dates being 3 May or 10 May. This speculation intensified when rumours circulated that Albanese might announce the election as early as Friday 28 March, for one of these dates. On Thursday 27 March, the [[Department of the Prime Minister and Cabinet (Australia)|Department of Prime Minister and Cabinet]] accidentally posted, then deleted, a message to social media platform X (formerly [[Twitter]]), referring to the government being in "[[Caretaker government of Australia|caretaker mode]]". This immediately fuelled speculation that the election would be called the very next morning. Several media outlets reported that they expected the election to be called for 3 May.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/federal-election-date-revealed/ratudffcu|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250330103612/https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/federal-election-date-revealed/ratudffcu|archive-date=30 March 2025|title=The federal election will be held on 3 May|first1=Anna|last1=Henderson|work=SBS News|date=27 March 2025|access-date=28 March 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-03-27/albanese-expected-to-call-federal-election-this-morning/105103954|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250404232539/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-03-27/albanese-expected-to-call-federal-election-this-morning/105103954|archive-date=4 April 2025|url-status=live|title=May 3 federal election to be called by Anthony Albanese Friday morning|first1=Jake|last1=Evans|work=ABC News|date=27 March 2025|access-date=28 March 2025}}</ref> As forecast, an election to be held on 3 May was called on 28 March, when Albanese visited [[Governor-General of Australia|Governor-General]] [[Sam Mostyn]] and advised her to prorogue Parliament and dissolve the House of Representatives,<ref name="Proclamation"/> which she did.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.legislation.gov.au/C2025G00157/asmade/text|url-status=live|archive-date=21 April 2025|title=Proclamation - Prorogue the Parliament and dissolve the House of Representatives|date=28 March 2025|access-date=21 April 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250421050637/https://www.legislation.gov.au/C2025G00157/asmade/text |via=Federal Register of Legislation |id=C2025G00157}}</ref>

==Election timeline==
[[File:Early voting centre at Pilgrim House in Civic April 2025.jpg|thumb|An early voting centre in Canberra on 24 April]]
The Australian Electoral Commission (AEC) has stated that in accordance with the ''[[Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918]]'', the key dates for this election are as follows:<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.aec.gov.au/election/fe25/key-dates.htm |access-date=28 March 2025 |title=The 2025 Federal Election |publisher=Australian Electoral Commission |date=9 April 2025 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250409234735/https://aec.gov.au/election/fe25/key-dates.htm |archive-date=9 April 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.aec.gov.au/About_AEC/Publications/files/AEC-Service-Plan.pdf |access-date=28 March 2025 |date=16 January 2025 |publisher=Australian Electoral Commission |page=5 |title=Delivering the 2025 Federal Election |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250316133121/https://www.aec.gov.au/About_AEC/Publications/files/AEC-Service-Plan.pdf |url-status=live |archive-date=16 March 2025 |work=Service Plan}}</ref><ref name="Proclamation">{{cite letter |archive-date=29 March 2025 |url-status=live |url=https://www.gg.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-03/20250328%20Documents%20relating%20to%20the%20calling%20of%20the%20Federal%20Election%20for%203%20May%202025.pdf |date=28 March 2025 |subject=Proclamation |id=MS25-000144 |location=Parliament House Canberra ACT |first=Anthony |last=Albanese |recipient=Governor-General [[Sam Mostyn]] |access-date=30 March 2025 |publisher=gg.gov.au |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250329230300/https://www.gg.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-03/20250328%20Documents%20relating%20to%20the%20calling%20of%20the%20Federal%20Election%20for%203%20May%202025.pdf}}</ref>
*Issue of [[Writ of election|writs]] – Monday 31 March
*Close of [[Electoral roll|rolls]] – 8pm, Monday 7 April
*Close of [[nomination]]s – 12pm, Thursday 10 April
*Declaration of nominations – 12pm, Friday 11 April
*Early voting opens – Tuesday 22 April
*Mobile voting opens – Tuesday 22 April
*Close of postal voting applications – 6pm, Wednesday 30 April
*Polling day (8am until 6pm) – Saturday, 3 May
*Final day for receipt of postal votes – Friday, 16 May
*Latest date for return of writs – Wednesday, 9 July

Schedule two of the ''Broadcasting Services Act 1992'' requires that from 12am on 1 May until 3 May when polls close at 6pm, the broadcasting of political statements on television is prohibited. This "blackout" period does not include advertising on social media, newspapers, text message or email communications, or streaming services.<ref>{{cite news |work=SBS News |access-date=2 May 2025 |url=https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/the-election-advertising-blackout-is-here-but-chances-are-you-wont-notice-much-of-a-difference/1ponucr8v |title=The election advertising blackout is here, but chances are you won't notice much of a difference |date=1 May 2025 |archive-date=2 May 2025 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250502013351/https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/the-election-advertising-blackout-is-here-but-chances-are-you-wont-notice-much-of-a-difference/1ponucr8v |at=Explainer |first=Alexandra |last=Koster}}</ref><ref>{{Cite Legislation AU|Cth|act|bsa1992214/sch2.html|Broadcasting Services Act 1992 Schedule 2}}{{cbignore}}</ref>

== Campaign ==
In early January 2025, both Albanese and Dutton made public appearances which were interpreted by the media as "unofficial" campaign launches. Albanese visited electorates in Queensland, the Northern Territory and Western Australia.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Jervis-Bardy |first=Dan |date=2025-01-05 |title=Anthony Albanese switches to election footing with blitz of three campaign battlegrounds |url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/jan/06/anthony-albanese-switches-to-election-footing-with-blitz-of-three-campaign-battlegrounds |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250329190746/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/jan/06/anthony-albanese-switches-to-election-footing-with-blitz-of-three-campaign-battlegrounds |url-status=live |archive-date=29 March 2025 |access-date=2025-01-12 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> Prominent campaign issues were housing, cost of living, healthcare, nuclear and renewable energies, immigration, the public sector, the [[National Disability Insurance Scheme]], and defence.<ref name="Chrysanthos 2025">{{cite news|first=Natassia|last=Chrysanthos|archive-url=https://archive.today/20250328123146/https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/the-key-issues-that-will-define-the-election-and-where-the-major-parties-stand-20250306-p5lhbw.html|archive-date=28 March 2025|url-status=live|url=https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/the-key-issues-that-will-define-the-election-and-where-the-major-parties-stand-20250306-p5lhbw.html|date=17 April 2025|title=Where Albanese and Dutton stand on the eight key issues in this election|website=The Sydney Morning Herald|access-date=6 May 2025}}</ref> Dutton gave a speech in Melbourne where he launched the Liberal party slogan for the campaign, "Let's get Australia back on track", and focused on the topics of nuclear power, housing, and immigration.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Jervis-Bardy |first=Dan |date=2025-01-12 |title=What we learned – and didn't – from Peter Dutton's unofficial campaign launch |url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/jan/12/what-we-learned-and-didnt-from-peter-duttons-unoffical-campaign-launch |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250329042440/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/jan/12/what-we-learned-and-didnt-from-peter-duttons-unoffical-campaign-launch |url-status=live |archive-date=29 March 2025 |access-date=2025-01-12 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> He ran what was considered by numerous commentators to be a poor campaign.<ref>{{cite web |last=Savva |first=Niki |authorlink=Niki Savva |date=29 April 2025 |title=Dutton has led one of the worst election campaigns in living memory |url=https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/dutton-has-led-one-of-the-worst-election-campaigns-in-living-memory-20250429-p5lv7q.html |accessdate=4 May 2025 |work=[[The Sydney Morning Herald]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Tingle |first=Laura |authorlink=Laura Tingle |date=4 May 2025 |title=This election result shows Labor learned a lesson that the Coalition did not |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-05-04/election-result-labor-victory-positive-future/105247606 |accessdate=4 May 2025 |work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Melzer |first=Max |date=4 May 2025 |title=Veteran journalist Chris Uhlmann, Sky News' Andrew Clennell deliver brutal verdict on Coalition's 'worst' ever campaign and 'approaches to policy' |url=https://www.skynews.com.au/australia-news/politics/veteran-journalist-chris-uhlmann-sky-news-andrew-clennell-deliver-brutal-verdict-on-coalitions-worst-ever-campaign-approaches-to-policy/news-story/a49126bd45cfb3998df2aa8983d18d5b |accessdate=4 May 2025 |work=[[Sky News Australia]]}}</ref>

On 23 February, Albanese announced that, if re-elected, Labor would invest an additional $8.5 billion into [[Medicare (Australia)|Medicare]] to improve [[bulk-billing]] rates, and reduce the maximum price that consumers pay for many PBS ([[Pharmaceutical Benefits Scheme]]) medications.<ref>{{cite news |work=SBS News |title=Billions pledged: What the parties are promising for your health this election |date=12 April 2025 |first=Julia |last=Abbondanza |url-status=live |archive-date=19 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250419130112/https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/health-promises-federal-election-2025/2su8r3qpe |url=https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/health-promises-federal-election-2025/2su8r3qpe}}</ref> On 25 March, treasurer [[Jim Chalmers]] delivered the [[2025 Australian federal budget|budget]] speech. A range of measures were announced, including a modest income tax cut to apply to all Australian taxpayers.<ref name="Budget">{{Cite book |isbn=978-1-923278-13-4 |title=Building Australia's Future |date=March 2025 |url=https://budget.gov.au/content/overview/download/budget-overview.pdf |archive-date=26 March 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250326004736/https://budget.gov.au/content/overview/download/budget-overview.pdf |url-status=live |access-date=4 April 2025 |series=Budget 2025–26 |publisher=budget.gov.au |via=The Commonwealth of Australia}}</ref> The Coalition opposed the tax cuts, instead offering a policy to cut the [[fuel excise]] by 25 cents per litre.<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-03-27/tax-cuts-pass-senate-in-late-night-sitting/105100954 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250327030355/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-03-27/tax-cuts-pass-senate-in-late-night-sitting/105100954 |url-status=live |access-date=5 April 2025 |archive-date=27 March 2025 |title=Surprise tax cuts pass Senate but Coalition vows to repeal them |date=27 March 2025 |first=Isobel |last=Roe |quote=Tax cuts revealed in the federal budget have passed the Senate in a late-night sitting, with the support of the Greens and the crossbench, but not Coalition senators.}}</ref> Dutton pledged not to ‘lie his way to the [[The Lodge, Australia|Lodge]]’.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Convery |first1=Stephanie |last2=Kurmelovs |first2=Royce |last3=Convery (now) |first3=Stephanie |last4=Kurmelovs (earlier) |first4=Royce |date=2025-04-27 |title=Dutton pledges not to 'lie my way to the Lodge' – as it happened |url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2025/apr/27/australia-election-2025-live-updates-anthony-albanese-peter-dutton-debate-labor-liberal-cost-of-living-medicare-telehealth-nsw-vic-ntwnfb |access-date=2025-05-11 |work=the Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> Following the budget speech, the Greens announced a policy that aims to include environmental measures in the budget using 1% of total funds.<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-01/greens-environmental-policies-federal-election-2025/105124818 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250401141814/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-01/greens-environmental-policies-federal-election-2025/105124818 |archive-date=1 April 2025 |access-date=4 April 2025 |url-status=live |date=1 April 2025 |title=Greens to push for 1pc environmental allocation in federal budget}}</ref> The Coalition also promised to defund the [[Environmental Defenders Office]], stating it had become a 'political football'.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2025-03-28 |title=The key election promises from Labor and the Coalition |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-03-28/election-2025-key-promises-labor-coalition/104717394 |access-date=2025-05-11 |work=ABC News |language=en-AU}}</ref>

===Official campaign===
{{See also|2025 Australian federal election debates and forums}}
*'''28 March''': An election to be held on 3 May is announced, following Albanese's visit to Mostyn to advise her to prorogue Parliament and dissolve the House of Representatives. The Parliament was then prorogued and the House of Representatives was dissolved.<ref name=truu2025>{{cite web |last=Truu |first=Maani |title=Campaign kicks off ahead of May 3 federal election, with lines drawn on cost of living and energy |website=ABC News |date=28 March 2025 |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-03-28/federal-election-2025-campaign-may-3/104750332 |access-date=6 April 2025 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250330054418/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-03-28/federal-election-2025-campaign-may-3/104750332 |archive-date=30 March 2025}}</ref>
*'''29 March''': Albanese and Dutton both campaign in Brisbane. Albanese begins his campaign in Dutton's electorate of [[Division of Dickson|Dickson]], and Dutton begins in the [[Division of Brisbane]]. Two of Dutton's events are disrupted by climate protesters, and a protester heckles Albanese at one of his events.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Cox |first1=Lisa |last2=Rachwani |name-list-style=and |first2=Mostafa |date=2025-03-29 |title=Protesters turn up at Dutton's events despite secret travel plans – as it happened |url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2025/mar/29/election-2025-live-updates-australia-federal-anthony-albanese-peter-dutton-ntwnfb?filterKeyEvents=false#liveblog-navigation |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250330014548/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2025/mar/29/election-2025-live-updates-australia-federal-anthony-albanese-peter-dutton-ntwnfb?filterKeyEvents=false#liveblog-navigation |url-status=live |archive-date=30 March 2025 |access-date=29 March 2025 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> Labor pledged to outlaw forms of [[price gouging]] by supermarkets, while announcing that it would also adopt all of the recommendations of the [[Australian Competition and Consumer Commission|ACCC]]'s supermarkets inquiry.<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-03-29/labor-commits-to-outlawing-supermarket-price-gouging/105112494 |access-date=21 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250401074456/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-03-29/labor-commits-to-outlawing-supermarket-price-gouging/105112494 |url-status=live |archive-date=1 April 2025 |title=Labor sets election promise to outlaw supermarket price gouging, after inquiry could not substantiate allegations |first=Maani |last=Truu |date=30 March 2025 |orig-date=29 March 2025}}</ref>
*'''30 March''': Along with Western Australian premier [[Roger Cook (politician)|Roger Cook]], Albanese announced that a re-elected Labor government would seek to upgrade the [[St John of God Midland Public and Private Hospitals|St John of God Midland hospital]] in Perth via an investment of $200 million.<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-03-30/albanese-to-announce-upgrade-to-perth-hospital/105114656 |access-date=26 April 2025 |date=30 March 2025 |url-status=live |archive-date=2 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250402071028/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-03-30/albanese-to-announce-upgrade-to-perth-hospital/105114656 |title=Anthony Albanese announced multi-million-dollar joint upgrade to Midland Hospital}}</ref> Dutton back-tracked on a proposal to hold three referendums on removing citizenship from dual nationals who commit certain crimes, to recognise Indigenous Australians in the Constitution, and to create four-year terms for parliament. Also at this press conference, Dutton promised to fund a security camera system for an [[Assyrians in Australia|Assyrian]] mosque in western Sydney with $25,000.<ref>{{cite news |work=The Sydney Morning Herald |title=Dutton backtracks on two referendum ideas, digs in on deportation |first=Matthew |last=Knott |date=30 March 2025 |url=https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/dutton-backtracks-on-two-referendum-ideas-digs-in-on-deportation-20250330-p5lnkt.html |access-date=5 May 2025 |url-status=live |archive-date=30 March 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250330072259/https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/dutton-backtracks-on-two-referendum-ideas-digs-in-on-deportation-20250330-p5lnkt.html}}</ref>
*'''1 April''': The [[Reserve Bank of Australia]] announced it is keeping interest rates at 4.1%.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Commins |first=Patrick |date=1 April 2025 |title=RBA holds rates at 4.1% but Michele Bullock says room for cuts if global trade war takes toll |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250401131642/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/apr/01/rba-interest-rates-hold-today |url-status=live |archive-date=1 April 2025 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/apr/01/rba-interest-rates-hold-today |access-date=1 April 2025 |work=[[Guardian Australia|The Guardian]] |language=en-AU |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> [[Teal independent]] candidate for the [[Division of Bradfield|seat of Bradfield]] [[Nicolette Boele]] was reportedly banned from a hairdressing salon for making a sexualised joke to a 19-year-old hair washer.<ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-01/teal-independent-bradfield-nicolette-boele-apologises-hair-salon/105124018 |date=1 April 2025 | title=Teal candidate banned from Sydney salon after making crude remark | work=ABC News | last=Willan |first=Fiona | access-date=2 April 2025 | archive-date=1 April 2025 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250401081749/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-01/teal-independent-bradfield-nicolette-boele-apologises-hair-salon/105124018 | url-status=live }}</ref>
*'''3 April''': Albanese responds to [[Donald Trump]]'s "[[Liberation Day tariffs|Liberation Day]]" tariff on Australia's exported goods by unveiling a five-point plan to boost the local economy to counteract the United States' policy.<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-03/albanese-outlines-five-point-plan-trump-tariffs-election-2025/105131590 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250406051322/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-03/albanese-outlines-five-point-plan-trump-tariffs-election-2025/105131590 |archive-date=6 April 2025 |url-status=live |access-date=7 April 2025 |date=3 April 2025 |title=Albanese outlines five-point plan to respond to Donald Trump's tariffs |first=Samantha |last=Dick}}</ref>
*'''4 April''': Both major parties pledged to return the [[Port of Darwin]] to Australian ownership if elected.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-04/albanese-vows-to-return-port-of-darwin-to-australia-election/105140260 |title=Coalition and Labor pledge to retake control of Chinese-controlled Port of Darwin |first=Alison |last=Xiao |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250406073316/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-04/albanese-vows-to-return-port-of-darwin-to-australia-election/105140260 |archive-date=6 April 2025 |work=ABC News |date=4 April 2025 |access-date=6 April 2025}}</ref>
*'''6 April''': The Liberal Party disendorses its candidate for the [[Division of Whitlam]], Benjamin Britton, due to previous disparaging comments he made towards service by women in the military.<ref>{{cite news |work=[[news.com.au]] |url=https://www.news.com.au/national/politics/the-liberal-party-has-dumped-the-nsw-candidate-for-the-seat-of-whitlam-over-claims-women-shouldnt-be-in-the-army/news-story/56d1ad1f60c5a4dafef9e9c815d04007 |access-date=6 April 2025 |date=6 April 2025 |title=The Liberal Party has dumped the NSW candidate for the seat of Whitlam over claims women shouldn't be in the army |name-list-style=and |first1=Samantha |last1=Maiden |last2=Brown |first2=Natalie |author-link1=Samantha Maiden}}</ref> Dutton also announced a Coalition policy to cap international university student numbers to 240,000 and raise visa prices for students.<ref name="Student">{{cite news |work=[[SBS News]] |url=https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/coalition-to-cut-international-student-numbers-by-80-000-raise-visa-application-fees/nofupp8l5 |access-date=7 April 2025 |date=6 April 2025 |title=Coalition to cut international student numbers by 80,000, raise visa application fees |first=Cameron |last=Carr |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250407032120/https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/coalition-to-cut-international-student-numbers-by-80-000-raise-visa-application-fees/nofupp8l5 |archive-date=7 April 2025}}</ref> The Greens called this policy proposal a "cynical attempt to scapegoat migrants and international students".<ref name="Student"/> Dutton previously said that this policy proposal aims to get the "[[woke]]" out of the university and schooling system.<ref>{{cite news |work=[[The Conversation (website)|The Conversation]] |author-link=Michelle Grattan |first=Michelle |last=Grattan |access-date=7 April 2025 |url=https://theconversation.com/election-diary-dutton-flags-intervention-in-what-he-sees-as-woke-education-but-how-much-could-he-actually-do-253116 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250401105411/https://theconversation.com/election-diary-dutton-flags-intervention-in-what-he-sees-as-woke-education-but-how-much-could-he-actually-do-253116 |archive-date=1 April 2025 |url-status=live |title=Election Diary: Dutton flags intervention in what he sees as 'woke' education, but how much could he actually do? |date=1 April 2025}}</ref>
*'''7 April''': The Coalition [[U-turn (politics)|backflipped]] on a proposed policy to end [[work from home]] arrangements for the public sector workforce and dropped their demands for mass job cuts in the public sector.<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |title=Coalition abandons 'end' to work from home, walks back 41,000 job cuts |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-06/coalition-abandon-work-from-home-41000-jobs/105144090 |access-date=7 April 2025 |archive-date=7 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250407023837/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-06/coalition-abandon-work-from-home-41000-jobs/105144090 |date=7 April 2025 |orig-date=6 April 2025 |first1=Jake |last1=Evans |url-status=live |last2=Dalzell |first2=Stephanie |name-list-style=and}}</ref> Also on this day, the stock market fell sharply due to the risk of the [[Tariffs in the second Trump administration|tariffs imposed by the Trump administration]], with Chalmers stating that Australia is "uniquely placed" to deal with the impacts of these tariffs.<ref>{{cite news |work=The Guardian |access-date=7 April 2025 |date=7 April 2025 |title=Labor may use 'powers' to take back Port of Darwin, Marles says – as it happened |url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2025/apr/07/australia-election-2025-live-coalition-labor-federal-peter-dutton-anthony-albanese-job-cuts-redundancies-batteries-solar?page=with%3Ablock-67f372498f0843b305e4c60e#block-67f372498f0843b305e4c60e |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250407105155/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2025/apr/07/australia-election-2025-live-coalition-labor-federal-peter-dutton-anthony-albanese-job-cuts-redundancies-batteries-solar?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-67f379d88f082d3642a79bb4#block-67f379d88f082d3642a79bb4 |archive-date=7 April 2025 |url-status=live |at=What we learned, Monday 7 April |name-list-style=and |first1=Emily |last1=Wind |last2=Dhanji |first2=Krishani}}</ref>
*'''8 April''': Albanese was heckled by a climate protester when he was announcing a $1 billion investment for Medicare.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.thenewdaily.com.au/news/politics/australian-politics/federal-election-2025/2025/04/08/albanese-climate-protest-sydney |access-date=9 April 2025 |work=[[The New Daily]] |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250408235219/https://www.thenewdaily.com.au/news/politics/australian-politics/federal-election-2025/2025/04/08/albanese-climate-protest-sydney |archive-date=8 April 2025 |date=8 April 2025 |title=PM heckled, accused of 'condemning young people' |first1=Kat |last1=Wong |last2=Brown |first2=Andrew |name-list-style=and}}</ref> Also on this day, [[Sky News Australia|Sky News]] and ''[[The Daily Telegraph (Sydney)|The Daily Telegraph]]'' held the first leaders election debate between Albanese and Dutton in a People's Forum. Albanese was declared the winner of the Forum with 44 votes compared to 35 for Dutton, and 21 undecided votes out of a possible 100.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/gaza-nuclear-and-demonising-migrants-pm-declared-winner-over-dutton-in-debate-clash/ffxcpemw7 |work=SBS News |title=The Daily Telegraph and Sky News to host first election debate as 100 undecided voters put Albanese, Dutton to the test |access-date=9 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250408235330/https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/gaza-nuclear-and-demonising-migrants-pm-declared-winner-over-dutton-in-debate-clash/ffxcpemw7 |url-status=live |archive-date=8 April 2025 |date=8 April 2025 |quote=The prime minister was declared the winner of the first leader's debate, with 44 of the 100 voters in the room choosing Albanese. Thirty-five voted for Dutton and 21 were undecided.|first1=Ewa |last1=Staszewska|first2=Anna|last2=Henderson|first3=Sara|last3=Tomevska|first4=Naveen|last4=Razik}}</ref> Liberal and National party social media however incorrectly congratulated Dutton for winning the debate.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Butler |first=Josh |date=2025-04-09 |title='Congratulations Peter Dutton': LNP incorrectly declares leader election debate winner before audience votes for Anthony Albanese |url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/apr/09/election-leaders-debate-winner-albanese-social-media-posts-congratulate-peter-dutton |url-status=live |archive-date=11 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250411004237/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/apr/09/election-leaders-debate-winner-albanese-social-media-posts-congratulate-peter-dutton |access-date=2025-04-09 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref>
*'''9 April''': On a visit to [[Division of Leichhardt|Leichhardt]] in [[Far North Queensland]], Albanese announces that Labor would commit $490 million worth of funds to upgrade the [[Barron River Bridge]].<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250417221943/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-09/federal-election-far-north-queensland-kuranda-range-highway/105156822 |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-09/federal-election-far-north-queensland-kuranda-range-highway/105156822 |archive-date=17 April 2025 |url-status=live |access-date=30 April 2025 |title=Labor commits to fixing Barron River bridge but locals want new highway too |first1=Christopher |last1=Testa |last2=McManus |first2=Sophie |date=10 April 2025 |orig-date=9 April 2025 |name-list-style=and}}</ref> [[Sky News Australia|Sky News]] and ''[[The Daily Telegraph (Sydney)|The Daily Telegraph]]'' co-hosted the first treasurers election debate between Chalmers and shadow treasurer [[Angus Taylor]] in their second people's forum of the campaign.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.skynews.com.au/australia-news/politics/sky-news-australia-to-host-first-treasurers-debate-of-the-federal-election-campaign-between-jim-chalmers-and-angus-taylor-as-australians-battle-cost-of-living-crisis/news-story/47070d8a2a168ff8031996906727c81a|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250408042342/https://www.skynews.com.au/australia-news/politics/sky-news-australia-to-host-first-treasurers-debate-of-the-federal-election-campaign-between-jim-chalmers-and-angus-taylor-as-australians-battle-cost-of-living-crisis/news-story/47070d8a2a168ff8031996906727c81a|archive-date=8 April 2025|work=Sky News |title=Sky News Australia to host first Treasurers' debate of the Federal Election campaign between Jim Chalmers and Angus Taylor as Australians battle cost of living crisis |access-date=8 April 2025 |date=3 April 2025 |first=Tyrone |last=Clarke}}</ref> Chalmers opened the debate by highlighting the challenging economic circumstances faced by Labor upon taking office, and the steps they had taken to improve the situation since. Taylor opened with the Coalition's established line of attack, claiming that Australians were worse off than three years prior. Chalmers defended the government's fiscal record, pointing to a $207 billion improvement in the budget bottom line.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wind |last2=McLeod |first2=Catie |name-list-style=and |first1=Emily |title=Chalmers claims Coalition planning 'secret cuts' as Taylor guarantees health and education spending – as it happened |url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2025/apr/09/australia-election-2025-live-anthony-albanese-peter-dutton-labor-coalition-greens-adam-bandt-debate-gas-prices-cost-of-living-economy-tariffsntwnfb |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250412102943/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2025/apr/09/australia-election-2025-live-anthony-albanese-peter-dutton-labor-coalition-greens-adam-bandt-debate-gas-prices-cost-of-living-economy-tariffsntwnfb |archive-date=12 April 2025 |access-date=20 April 2025 |work=The Guardian |date=9 April 2025}}</ref> Energy policy, particularly gas, emerged as a major point of contention during the debate. The Coalition claimed its plan would reduce household gas bills by 7%, based on modelling conducted by Frontier Economics; however, the analysis was brief — comprising just 135 words on power prices — and was dismissed by climate change and energy minister [[Chris Bowen]] as a "scamphlet". Coalition energy spokesperson [[Ted O'Brien (Australian politician)|Ted O'Brien]] later acknowledged that any savings were "likely to be a lagging indicator," suggesting consumers might not experience immediate benefits.<ref name="guardian_post_treasurers_debate">{{cite news |last1=Visontay |first1=Elias |title=Afternoon Update Election 2025: Coalition gas 'scamphlet'; the 'losers' burning the world down; and why all the bogongs have gone |url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/apr/09/afternoon-update-wednesday-ntwnfb |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250411003521/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/apr/09/afternoon-update-wednesday-ntwnfb |archive-date=11 April 2025 |url-status=live |access-date=20 April 2025 |work=The Guardian |date=9 April 2025 |quote=The climate change and energy minister, Chris Bowen, condemned the Frontier Economics analysis as a "scamphlet", describing the 15-page document as "alleged modelling" that included only 135 words on how it would affect electricity prices.}}</ref>
*'''10 April''': The Coalition announced a policy to establish a regional Australia future fund to invest $20 billion in regional infrastructure and services, the fund would be topped-up using [[Commodity market|commodity]] royalties, and another fund to pay down debt and finance infrastructure projects. They have proposed abolishing a scheme that Labor set up in government to build renewable energy infrastructure. They have also proposed ending the housing Australia future fund and the national reconstruction fund.<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-10/election-20-billion-regional-fund/105156176 |access-date=20 April 2025 |archive-date=10 April 2025 |title=Coalition pledges $20b Regional Australia Future Fund to improve health, child care and infrastructure inequalities |date=10 April 2025 |first=Lucy |last=Barbour |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250410093440/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-10/election-20-billion-regional-fund/105156176 |url-status=live}}</ref> Also on this day, a debate between the minister for climate change and energy [[Chris Bowen]] and his shadow counterpart Ted O'Brien took place at the [[National Press Club (Australia)|National Press Club]]. The event was hosted by Tom Connell of [[Sky News Australia|Sky News]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.skynews.com.au/australia-news/politics/national-press-club-energy-ministers-debate-to-be-hosted-by-sky-news-australias-tom-connell/video/1039e7172dbb3bfcd885efd68f96f9e5|work=Sky News |url-status=live |archive-date=8 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250408100150/https://www.skynews.com.au/australia-news/politics/national-press-club-energy-ministers-debate-to-be-hosted-by-sky-news-australias-tom-connell/video/1039e7172dbb3bfcd885efd68f96f9e5 |title=National Press Club energy ministers' debate to be hosted by Sky News Australia's Tom Connell |access-date=8 April 2025 |date=3 April 2025}}</ref> During O'Brien's opening remarks, he was interrupted by a climate change activist.<ref>{{cite news |work=SBS News |url=https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/bowen-and-obrien-face-off-over-price-of-power-bills/5nvt5s6v0 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250412044853/https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/bowen-and-obrien-face-off-over-price-of-power-bills/5nvt5s6v0 |url-status=live |archive-date=12 April 2025 |access-date=19 April 2025 |title='Happy for Australia to go broke': Major parties face off over price of power bills |date=10 April 2025 |first=Ewa |last=Staszewska}}</ref> The Coalition also announced a policy to repeal penalties for the sale of fuel-inefficient vehicles, instead proposing to re-work the law.<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-10/coalition-to-dump-fuel-efficiency-laws/105162808 |title=Coalition to abolish fuel efficiency penalties, dubbing them 'unfair tax' |date=10 April 2025 |access-date=23 April 2025 |archive-date=23 April 2025 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250423100833/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-10/coalition-to-dump-fuel-efficiency-laws/105162808 |first=Jake |last=Evans}}</ref>
*'''12 April''': Price accused the media of being "obsessed with [[Donald Trump]]" and vowed to "make Australia great again" in a speech to Liberal party supporters.<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-12/jacinta-nampijinpa-price-trump-make-australia-great-again/105169348 |access-date=3 May 2025 |date=13 April 2025 |orig-date=12 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250501194127/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-12/jacinta-nampijinpa-price-trump-make-australia-great-again/105169348 |archive-date=1 May 2025 |url-status=live |first=Maani |last=Truu |title=Jacinta Nampijinpa Price vows to 'make Australia great again', accuses media of being 'Trump obsessed'}}</ref>
*'''13 April''': Labor and the Coalition held their official campaign launches. Labor's was in [[Perth]], and the Coalition's was in [[Liverpool, New South Wales|Liverpool]] in [[western Sydney]]. The Labor party announced several new policies such as a $1,000 tax deduction for work-related expenses and that the government would fund 100,000 new homes exclusively for first-home buyers with a $10 billion investment, and an expansion of the [[Help to Buy|help to buy]] scheme. The Coalition announced that they would allow first-home buyers who purchase new builds to deduct interest from their mortgage payments on the first $650,000 for five years, with the proposed scheme being [[Means test|means-tested]]. Dutton recommitted the party to a previously announced policy to access up to $50,000 of their [[Superannuation in Australia|superannuation]] to purchase a first-home. Dutton announced a tax break to counter cost-of-living pressures which would apply to those earning up to $144,000, with Dutton claiming that the policy would mean these earners are $1,200 at the end of the year.<ref>{{cite news |work=SBS News |title=Campaigns launched: PM pledges $1k 'instant tax deduction', Dutton teases defence boost |url=https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/labor-and-the-coalition-launch-their-2025-federal-election-campaigns/a8obe1vdm |url-status=live |access-date=19 April 2025 |archive-date=19 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250419125834/https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/labor-and-the-coalition-launch-their-2025-federal-election-campaigns/a8obe1vdm |date=13 April 2025 |first=David |last=Aidone}}</ref>
*'''14 April''': Labor announced a $10 million investment to improve medical services for [[LGBTQ|LGBTQIA+]] people.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/apr/14/labor-announces-10m-to-provide-inclusive-culturally-safe-healthcare-for-lgbtq-australians |work=The Guardian |access-date=23 April 2025 |archive-date=23 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250423121735/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/apr/14/labor-announces-10m-to-provide-inclusive-culturally-safe-healthcare-for-lgbtq-australians |url-status=live |date=14 April 2025 |title=Labor announces $10m to provide 'inclusive, culturally safe' healthcare for LGBTQ+ Australians |first=Josh |last=Butler}}</ref> A [[Family First Party (2021)|Family First]] candidate contesting the election for [[Division of Longman|Longman]] in [[Queensland]], Malachi Brogden Hearne, was disendorsed by the party's leadership due to his derogatory posts on social media.<ref>{{cite news |work=6 News Australia |title=Family First candidate disendorsed over social media posts |url=https://www.6newsau.com/post/family-first-candidate-disendorsed-over-social-media-posts |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250418205428/https://www.6newsau.com/post/family-first-candidate-disendorsed-over-social-media-posts |url-status=live |archive-date=18 April 2025 |access-date=30 April 2025 |date=15 April 2025 |orig-date=14 April 2025 |first=Leonardo |last=Puglisi}}</ref>
*'''15 April''': Labor announced a $3.8 million package to keep a Canberra health centre from falling into administration.<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-15/act-labor-pledges-to-fund-health-co-op-canberra/105179780 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250417045233/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-15/act-labor-pledges-to-fund-health-co-op-canberra/105179780 |archive-date=17 April 2025 |url-status=live |access-date=29 April 2025 |title=Reprieve for Canberra bulk-billing health service as private operator steps in |date=15 April 2025}}</ref> The military information website ''[[Jane's Defence Weekly|Janes]]'' claimed that [[Russia]] was interested in setting up an aircraft base in [[Indonesia]]. However, the Indonesian foreign minister stridently denied this claim. Dutton said it would be a "catastrophic failure" of diplomacy if Albanese and foreign minister [[Penny Wong]] did not combat this issue.<ref>{{cite news |work=The Sydney Morning Herald |url=https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/vladimir-putin-seeks-access-to-indonesian-air-force-base-reports-20250415-p5lrvb.html |url-access=subscription |access-date=21 April 2025 |date=15 April 2025 |first1=Matthew |last1=Knott |last2=Rompies |first2=Karuni |title='Simply not true': Indonesia rejects reports of Russian military base |archive-date=15 April 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250415084456/https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/vladimir-putin-seeks-access-to-indonesian-air-force-base-reports-20250415-p5lrvb.html |url-status=live}}</ref> Also on this day, Greens leader [[Adam Bandt]] announced policies that would be a priority for the party if the election results in a hung parliament. These policies would include aiming to get dental into Medicare, lowering the price of child care, ending native forest logging, and Bandt reiterated a policy to wind back some tax concessions.<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-15/federal-election-2025-live-updates-albanese-dutton-campaign/105175764 |title=Federal Election 2025: Bandt says a Green Brisbane is the key to keeping 'Dutton out' — as it happened |access-date=21 April 2025 |date=15 April 2025 |name-list-style=and |first1=Courtney |last1=Gould |last2=Hewson |first2=Georgie |last3=Boscaini |first3=Joshua |first4=Andrew |last4=Thorpe |first5=Paul |last5=Johnson |at=Greensland key to keeping Dutton out of PM's office |archive-date=17 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250417162144/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-15/federal-election-2025-live-updates-albanese-dutton-campaign/105175764 |url-status=live }}</ref>
*'''16 April''': A Greens candidate for the [[division of Franklin]] in [[Tasmania]] ceases campaigning due to revelations that he holds dual citizenship. As nominations had already closed he would still appear on the ballot paper as a Greens member.<ref>{{cite news |work=SBS News |url=https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/greens-candidate-forced-to-withdraw-from-election-over-disgraceful-citizenship-rule/k7b8m0des |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250424061240/https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/greens-candidate-forced-to-withdraw-from-election-over-disgraceful-citizenship-rule/k7b8m0des |archive-date=24 April 2025 |url-status=live |access-date=30 April 2025 |date=16 April 2025 |title=Greens candidate forced to withdraw from election over 'disgraceful' citizenship rule |first=Ewa |last=Staszewska}}</ref> The [[Australian Broadcasting Corporation]] (ABC) held a leaders debate between Albanese and Dutton moderated by ''[[Insiders (Australian TV program)|Insiders]]'' host [[David Speers]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-03/abc-to-host-election-leaders-debate/105130036 |access-date=6 April 2025 |title=ABC to host election leaders' debate on April 16 |date=3 April 2025 |work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250406072840/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-03/abc-to-host-election-leaders-debate/105130036 |url-status=live |archive-date=6 April 2025}}</ref>
*'''17 April''': Dutton announced a long-term aspiration to [[Indexation|index]] tax brackets.<ref>{{cite news |work=The Conversation |url=https://theconversation.com/grattan-on-friday-peter-duttons-tax-indexation-aspiration-has-merit-so-why-didnt-we-hear-about-it-before-254589 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250421030401/https://theconversation.com/grattan-on-friday-peter-duttons-tax-indexation-aspiration-has-merit-so-why-didnt-we-hear-about-it-before-254589 |url-status=live |archive-date=21 April 2025 |access-date=21 April 2025 |date=17 April 2025 |first=Michelle |last=Grattan |title=Grattan on Friday: Peter Dutton's tax indexation 'aspiration' has merit – so why didn't we hear about it before?}}</ref> Albanese has criticised this aspiration.<ref>{{cite news |via=Yahoo! News |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250420100511/https://au.news.yahoo.com/pressure-dutton-russia-mistake-203049739.html |archive-date=20 April 2025 |url-status=live |url=https://au.news.yahoo.com/pressure-dutton-russia-mistake-203049739.html |work=[[NCA NewsWire|NewsWire]] |first1=Liam |last1=Beatty |last2=Keoghan |first2=Sarah |last3=Jackson |first3=Blair |date=18 April 2025 |name-list-style=and |title='Laughable': Dutton's tax aspiration slammed}}</ref> Later on this day, a debate took place on the ABC's [[7.30]] program between housing minister [[Clare O'Neil]] and shadow housing minister [[Michael Sukkar]].<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-17/federal-election-debate-housing-policy-negative-gearing/105162886 |access-date=21 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250421082202/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-17/federal-election-debate-housing-policy-negative-gearing/105162886 |url-status=live |archive-date=21 April 2025 |name-list-style=and |first1=Kate |last1=Ainsworth |last2=Johnson |first2=Paul |title=Clare O'Neil and Michael Sukkar clash over housing policy and trade claims of lying in fiery live debate |date=17 April 2025}}</ref>
*'''19 April''': [[Trumpet of Patriots]] held their official campaign launch in Queensland. The party proposed a department of government efficiency and cutting immigration. Chairman of the party [[Clive Palmer]] proposed abolishing [[Net-zero emissions|net-zero]] targets set as per the [[Paris Climate Agreement]]. The party has also proposed building [[High-speed rail in Australia|high-speed rail]] and capping [[interest rate]]s at 3%.<ref>{{cite news |work=[[Australian Associated Press]] |access-date=19 April 2025 |archive-date=19 April 2025 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20250419085505/https://au.news.yahoo.com/horn-blows-trumpet-patriots-campaign-173000915.html?guccounter=1&guce_referrer=aHR0cHM6Ly9kdWNrZHVja2dvLmNvbS8&guce_referrer_sig=AQAAAIrEPKOGUHwL8wVbYkvsbJb_Udp9uYSljk9vZQXPkRoGAvz5ZvKB6awdPAspkE9mPvjCjR0Ouc7o_dO7utNLLFOVxPntEo2HFidLz_KYjQ1HS3LFF4t8xHWT9fd7s-3QmoN1lwY9CDVRB2ROlE7tIEDoxNmJcURn3YjXclkN9QHp |url=https://au.news.yahoo.com/horn-blows-trumpet-patriots-campaign-173000915.html?guccounter=1&guce_referrer=aHR0cHM6Ly9kdWNrZHVja2dvLmNvbS8&guce_referrer_sig=AQAAAIrEPKOGUHwL8wVbYkvsbJb_Udp9uYSljk9vZQXPkRoGAvz5ZvKB6awdPAspkE9mPvjCjR0Ouc7o_dO7utNLLFOVxPntEo2HFidLz_KYjQ1HS3LFF4t8xHWT9fd7s-3QmoN1lwY9CDVRB2ROlE7tIEDoxNmJcURn3YjXclkN9QHp |via=[[Yahoo News|Yahoo! News]] |title=Palmer makes pitch to Patriots at campaign launch |first=Fraser |last=Barton |date=19 April 2025}}</ref> Also on this day, Albanese announced that Labor would enshrine [[penalty rates]] via legislation.<ref>{{cite news |work=The Guardian |url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/apr/19/labor-vows-to-protect-penalty-rates-and-seeks-to-reignite-fight-over-working-from-home |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250419134542/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/apr/19/labor-vows-to-protect-penalty-rates-and-seeks-to-reignite-fight-over-working-from-home |archive-date=19 April 2025 |access-date=21 April 2025 |date=19 April 2025 |first=Tory |last=Shepherd |url-status=live |title=Labor vows to protect penalty rates and seeks to reignite fight over working from home}}</ref>
*'''20 April''': The Greens announced their requests during negotiation in a [[hung parliament]], which include seeking changes to [[Negative gearing in Australia|negative gearing]] and the [[Capital gains tax in Australia|capital gains tax]].<ref>{{cite news |work=SBS News |url=https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/greens-put-negative-gearing-in-sights-in-minority-government/suiqygnpu |access-date=20 April 2025 |archive-date=20 April 2025 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250420082914/https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/greens-put-negative-gearing-in-sights-in-minority-government/suiqygnpu |title='Diffusing the timebomb': Greens put negative gearing in sights in minority government |date=20 April 2025 |first=Madeleine |last=Wedesweiler}}</ref> Also on this day, the former secretary of the [[Department of Home Affairs (Australia)|department of home affairs]] [[Mike Pezzullo]] was floated for reappointment into a public service role by Dutton.<ref>{{cite news |work=The Sydney Morning Herald |url=https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/dutton-says-sacked-home-affairs-boss-pezzullo-should-have-a-role-to-play-in-nation-s-future-20250420-p5lsz7.html |url-access=subscription |date=20 April 2025 |title=Dutton says sacked Home Affairs boss Pezzullo 'should have a role to play' in nation's future |access-date=21 April 2025 |name-list-style=and |first1=Deborah |last1=Snow |last2=Sakaal |first2=Paul |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250420095000/https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/dutton-says-sacked-home-affairs-boss-pezzullo-should-have-a-role-to-play-in-nation-s-future-20250420-p5lsz7.html |archive-date=20 April 2025}}</ref>
*'''21 April''': The Coalition announced a policy to trial a [[sex offender registry]]; while Dutton announced a further investment to fight crime.<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |access-date=23 April 2025 |archive-date=23 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250423102449/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-21/coalition-promises-child-sex-offender-register/105196454 |url-status=live |date=21 April 2025 |title=Coalition promises trial of child sex offender register |first=Jake |last=Evans |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-21/coalition-promises-child-sex-offender-register/105196454}}</ref> Jason Smart, a Trumpet of Patriots candidate for the [[division of Flinders]] in [[Victoria (state)|Victoria]] resigned from the party due to disagreements with its leadership but would still appear on the ballot paper due to nominations having previously closed.<ref>{{cite news |work=[[6 News Australia]] |url=https://www.6newsau.com/post/trumpet-of-patriots-candidate-withdraws-after-teal-independent-placed-second-how-to-vote-card |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250426045159/https://www.6newsau.com/post/trumpet-of-patriots-candidate-withdraws-after-teal-independent-placed-second-how-to-vote-card |archive-date=26 April 2025 |url-status=live |access-date=30 April 2025 |first=Leonardo |date=21 April 2025 |last=Puglisi |author-link=Leonardo Puglisi |title=Trumpet of Patriots candidate withdraws after teal independent placed second how-to-vote card}}</ref>
*'''21 April''': Following the [[death of Pope Francis]], both Albanese and Dutton briefly suspended their campaigns, as a mark of respect.<ref>{{cite news |work=The Guardian |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/apr/22/australians-pay-tribute-to-pope-francis |title='He stood for love and peace': Australian parishioners and politicians pay tribute to Pope Francis |access-date=25 April 2025 |date=22 April 2025 |first1=Adeshola |last1=Ore |last2=Touma |first2=Rafqa |name-list-style=and |url-status=live |archive-date=25 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250425025508/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/apr/22/australians-pay-tribute-to-pope-francis}}</ref> Labor announced that they would fund a Canberra-based aged care facility with $10 million following the ACT government's announcement that it would close.<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-22/act-federal-labor-commits-additional-aged-care-respite-funding/105202176 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250423031211/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-22/act-federal-labor-commits-additional-aged-care-respite-funding/105202176 |archive-date=23 April 2025 |date=22 April 2025 |title=Federal Labor commits to additional $10 million for aged care respite beds in Canberra after facility closure announced |access-date=29 April 2025 |first1=Charlotte |last1=Gore |last2=Frost |first2=Harry}}</ref> Labor announced a strategy to prevent domestic violence through a range of responses to stop perpetrators from hijacking a victim's finances.<ref>{{cite news |at=What has Labor pledged? |work=The Conversation |url=https://theconversation.com/the-major-parties-have-announced-their-plans-to-address-domestic-and-family-violence-how-do-they-stack-up-255127 |title=The major parties have announced their plans to address domestic and family violence. How do they stack up? |access-date=25 April 2025 |date=24 April 2025 |archive-date=24 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250424033304/https://theconversation.com/the-major-parties-have-announced-their-plans-to-address-domestic-and-family-violence-how-do-they-stack-up-255127 |url-status=live |first1=Kate |last1=Fitz-Gibbon |last2=Boxall |first2=Hayley}}</ref> Later, the [[Nine Network]] held a leaders debate between Albanese and Dutton moderated by ''[[A Current Affair (Australian TV program)|A Current Affair]]'' presenter [[Allison Langdon|Ally Langdon]] and with the leaders questioned by [[Charles Croucher]], Deb Knight and [[Phil Coorey]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-04-14 |title=Nine to host third leaders' debate between Anthony Albanese and Peter Dutton |first=Daniel |last=Jeffrey |url=https://www.9news.com.au/national/federal-election-2025-nine-hosts-third-leaders-debate/ced650b8-a4e2-483f-ae84-3adc5200dd8e |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250421085954/https://www.9news.com.au/national/federal-election-2025-nine-hosts-third-leaders-debate/ced650b8-a4e2-483f-ae84-3adc5200dd8e |archive-date=21 April 2025 |access-date=2025-04-14 |website=[[9news.com.au]]}}</ref>
*'''23 April''': The Coalition announced a policy to increase defence spending to 2.5% of [[GDP]] within five years, and then to 3% within the next decade.<ref>{{cite news |access-date=23 April 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250423100624/https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/coalition-pledges-to-increase-australia-defence-spend/6dk1b3kbs |url=https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/coalition-pledges-to-increase-australia-defence-spend/6dk1b3kbs |date=23 April 2025 |first=Sara |last=Tomevska |work=SBS News |url-status=live |archive-date=23 April 2025 |title=Coalition pledges to increase Australia's defence spend to 3 per cent of GDP}}</ref> The Coalition later announced a policy to repeal tax breaks for electric vehicles, in addition to student debt relief and production tax credits.<ref>{{cite news |work=The Sydney Morning Herald |url=https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/dutton-reverses-pledge-on-ev-tax-break-two-days-after-he-said-he-would-keep-it-20250423-p5lttb.html |access-date=23 April 2025 |date=23 April 2025 |name-list-style=and |first1=David |last1=Crowe |first2=Shane |last2=Wright |title=Dutton reverses pledge on EV tax break two days after saying he would keep it |archive-date=23 April 2025 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250423/https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/dutton-reverses-pledge-on-ev-tax-break-two-days-after-he-said-he-would-keep-it-20250423-p5lttb.html |url-access=subscription}}</ref> Also on this day, the Labor Party announced that they would fund a scheme designed to fast-track qualifications in order to help people get into [[Tradesperson|trades work]] sooner.<ref>{{cite news |work=7News |url=https://7news.com.au/news/election-battle-returns-to-the-home-front-with-labor-tradies-pledge-c-18454562 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250423125452/https://7news.com.au/news/election-battle-returns-to-the-home-front-with-labor-tradies-pledge-c-18454562 |archive-date=23 April 2025 |url-status=live |title=Election battle returns to the home front with Labor tradies pledge |date=23 April 2025 |access-date=23 April 2025 |first=Josh |last=Martin}}</ref> The Coalition announced a pledge to create a database of [[Domestic violence in Australia|domestic violence]] offenders in a package worth $90 million; part of this package also includes a [[royal commission]] investigating sexual abuse in Indigenous communities.<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-23/coalition-domestic-violence-package/105208720 |access-date=23 April 2025 |date=23 April 2025 |archive-date=23 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250423133144/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-23/coalition-domestic-violence-package/105208720 |title=Coalition unveils $90m domestic violence strategy focused on prevention, early intervention and crisis response |first=Evelyn |last=Manfield |url-status=live}}</ref> Details of a Coalition policy to create a special envoy position for jailed Australians abroad were revealed by ''The Sydney Morning Herald''.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/living-through-hell-coalition-pledge-to-rescue-jailed-australians-20250423-p5ltqs.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250423161040/https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/living-through-hell-coalition-pledge-to-rescue-jailed-australians-20250423-p5ltqs.html |archive-date=23 April 2025 |title='Living through hell': Coalition pledge to rescue jailed Australians |work=The Sydney Morning Herald |first=Matthew |last=Knott |url-access=subscription |date=23 April 2025 |access-date=25 April 2025}}</ref>
*'''24 April''': Albanese announced that Labor would create a strategic minerals reserve to be fully operational by late 2026 through an investment of $1.2 billion.<ref>{{cite news |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250424232834/https://www.reuters.com/markets/commodities/australias-albanese-pledges-set-up-critical-minerals-strategic-reserve-2025-04-23/ |url-status=live |archive-date=24 April 2025 |title=Australia's Albanese pledges to set up critical minerals strategic reserve |work=Reuters |date=24 April 2025 |access-date=25 April 2025 |first=Renju |last=Jose |editor-first=Sonali |editor-last=Paul |location=[[Sydney]]  |url=https://www.reuters.com/markets/commodities/australias-albanese-pledges-set-up-critical-minerals-strategic-reserve-2025-04-23/}}</ref> The Coalition confirmed their plan to cut over 41,000 public service workers would be limited to Canberra.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2025-04-24 |title=Public service cuts only for Canberra, Dutton says, which Labor calls 'impossible' |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-24/dutton-confirms-public-service-cuts-limited-to-canberra/105211946 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250424165419/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-24/dutton-confirms-public-service-cuts-limited-to-canberra/105211946 |archive-date=24 April 2025 |url-status=live |name-list-style=and |first1=Patrick |last1=Bell |last2=Crowley |first2=Tom |access-date=2025-04-24 |work=ABC News |language=en-AU}}</ref> The [[Australian Electoral Commission]] announced that more than 1.7 million Australians have already voted at pre-polling centres.<ref>{{Cite news |first=Ahmed |last=Yussuf |date=2025-04-25 |quote=By Thursday night, more than 1.78 million people had cast their ballots, according to the Australian Electoral Commission. |title=With more than a million early votes, experts say this is the new normal |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-25/more-than-a-million-early-votes-expert-say-new-normal/105211806 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250425031707/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-25/more-than-a-million-early-votes-expert-say-new-normal/105211806 |archive-date=25 April 2025 |url-status=live |access-date=2025-04-25 |work=ABC News}}</ref> William Bay, a candidate for the Great Australian Party running for the Senate in Queensland resigned from the party due to his disapproval of the party leadership's actions regarding the use of money and undermining of his campaign. As nominations had already closed, he would still appear on the ballot paper as a Great Australian Party candidate.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.6newsau.com/post/great-australian-party-candidate-quits-over-undermining-of-campaign |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250426045027/https://www.6newsau.com/post/great-australian-party-candidate-quits-over-undermining-of-campaign |archive-date=26 April 2025 |url-status=live |work=6 News Australia |access-date=30 April 2025 |date=24 April 2025 |title=Great Australian Party candidate quits over 'undermining of campaign' |first=Leonardo |last=Puglisi}}</ref>
*'''25 April''': The leaders paused campaigning in order to make time for [[Anzac Day]] commemorations.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/australians-anzac-day-services-across-country/446g9gcje |access-date=25 April 2025 |archive-date=25 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250425032244/https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/australians-anzac-day-services-across-country/446g9gcje |url-status=live |date=25 April 2025 |work=SBS News |agency=[[Australian Associated Press|AAP]] |title=In pictures: Thousands of Australians mark Anzac Day at events across the country |at=Federal leaders pause campaign to reflect}}</ref>
*'''26 April''': Albanese committed the Labor party to funding schools with a further $25 million to maintain knowledge of under-represented languages in Australian society.<ref>{{cite news |work=SBS News |url=https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/labor-promises-to-invest-25-million-in-new-language-schools/0hfl1cpfa |access-date=26 April 2025 |archive-date=26 April 2025 |date=26 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250426113210/https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/labor-promises-to-invest-25-million-in-new-language-schools/0hfl1cpfa |url-status=live |first=Naveen |last=Razik |title=Labor promises to invest $25 million in teaching students community languages}}</ref> Dutton said he would seek to unite people "under one flag", while stating that he thinks respect should be shown for [[Welcome to Country]] ceremonies following an incident at an Anzac Day event.<ref>{{cite news |work=[[The Nightly]] |access-date=26 April 2025 |date=26 April 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250426115358/https://thenightly.com.au/politics/federal-election-2025/liberal-leader-peter-dutton-calls-for-respect-of-welcome-to-country-c-18491595 |url-status=live |archive-date=26 April 2025 |title=Liberal leader Peter Dutton calls for respect of Welcome to Country |first=Katina |last=Curtis |url=https://thenightly.com.au/politics/federal-election-2025/liberal-leader-peter-dutton-calls-for-respect-of-welcome-to-country-c-18491595}}</ref> Albanese said that he would convene a meeting with representatives from the media after the election to discuss issues relating to the spread of extremist material.<ref>{{cite news |work=The Guardian |url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/apr/26/peter-dutton-urges-respect-for-welcome-to-country-but-reaffirms-stance-on-using-one-flag-ntwnfb |access-date=26 April 2025 |date=26 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250426121252/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/apr/26/peter-dutton-urges-respect-for-welcome-to-country-but-reaffirms-stance-on-using-one-flag-ntwnfb |title=Peter Dutton urges respect for welcome to country but reaffirms stance on 'one flag' only |archive-date=26 April 2025 |url-status=live |first1=Benita |last1=Kolovos |author2=Australian Associated Press}}</ref> [[Fatima Payman]] held the campaign launch for her party, [[Australia's Voice]], in [[Bankstown, New South Wales]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mahoney |first=John |date=2025-04-26 |title=Senator Payman launches Australia's Voice in NSW with powerful call for change |url=https://www.amust.com.au/2025/04/senator-payman-launches-australias-voice-in-nsw-with-powerful-call-for-change/ |archive-date=28 April 2025 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250428080818/https://www.amust.com.au/2025/04/senator-payman-launches-australias-voice-in-nsw-with-powerful-call-for-change/ |access-date=2025-04-28 |website=Australasian Muslim Times |language=en-AU}}</ref>
*'''27 April''': Albanese announced a policy to expand Medicare after-hours care via an investment of $204 million.<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |orig-date=26 April 2025 |date=27 April 2025 |title=Labor promises free after-hours telehealth in further Medicare pledge, rebranding existing hotline |first1=Jane |last1=Norman |last2=Manfield |first2=Evelyn |name-list-style=and |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-26/labor-promises-free-health-advice-hotline/105219694 |access-date=27 April 2025 |archive-date=26 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250426125354/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-26/labor-promises-free-health-advice-hotline/105219694 |url-status=live}}</ref> The Coalition announced that they consider charging electric vehicle users a road charge to compensate for the loss of fuel levies.<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-27/coalition-puts-ev-drivers-on-notice-over-road-use-tax/105221034 |access-date=27 April 2025 |archive-date=27 April 2025 |date=27 April 2025 |first=Tom |last=Crowley |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250427023347/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-27/coalition-puts-ev-drivers-on-notice-over-road-use-tax/105221034 |title=Coalition puts EV drivers on notice over road use tax |url-status=live}}</ref> At a Liberal party event in Victoria, Dutton branded ''The Guardian'' and ABC News as "hate media".<ref>{{cite news |work=The Guardian |url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/apr/27/peter-dutton-criticised-as-illiberal-for-calling-abc-and-the-guardian-hate-media-ntwnfb |access-date=28 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250427124657/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/apr/27/peter-dutton-criticised-as-illiberal-for-calling-abc-and-the-guardian-hate-media-ntwnfb |url-status=live |archive-date=27 April 2025 |first1=Ben |last1=Smee |last2=Basford Canales |first2=Sarah |date=27 April 2025 |title=Peter Dutton criticised as 'illiberal' for calling ABC and the Guardian 'hate media'}}</ref> The [[Seven Network]] held a leaders debate between Albanese and Dutton moderated by [[Seven News]] reporter [[Mark Riley (journalist)|Mark Riley]] and hosted by ''[[Sunrise (Australian TV program)|Sunrise]]'' presenter [[Natalie Barr]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Knox |first=David |date=2025-04-14 |title=Seven to screen final Leaders' Debate |url=https://tvtonight.com.au/2025/04/seven-to-screen-final-leaders-debate.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250419063423/https://tvtonight.com.au/2025/04/seven-to-screen-final-leaders-debate.html |archive-date=19 April 2025 |access-date=2025-04-14 |website=[[TV Tonight]] |language=en-AU}}</ref> A panel of 60 undecided voters assembled by pollster [[Roy Morgan]] declared Albanese the overall winner of the debate with 50% of the votes, with Dutton winning 25% of the votes and 25% remaining undecided; several other topics were also polled.<ref>{{cite news |at=1. Albanese was voted the winner |url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/apr/27/who-won-the-final-leaders-debate-seven-takeaways-from-albanese-v-dutton |access-date=27 April 2025 |date=27 April 2025 |first=Josh |last=Butler |work=The Guardian |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250427124629/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/apr/27/who-won-the-final-leaders-debate-seven-takeaways-from-albanese-v-dutton |archive-date=27 April 2025 |url-status=live |title=Who won the final leaders' debate? Seven takeaways from Albanese v Dutton}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |work=TV Blackbox |url-status=live |archive-date=28 April 2025 |access-date=28 April 2025 |date=28 April 2025 |title=Albanese scores victory in SEVEN'S Leaders' Debate ahead of election day |first=Kyle |last=Laidlaw |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250428020600/https://tvblackbox.com.au/page/2025/04/28/albanese-claims-victory-in-sevens-leaders-debate-ahead-of-election-day/ |url=https://tvblackbox.com.au/page/2025/04/28/albanese-claims-victory-in-sevens-leaders-debate-ahead-of-election-day/}}</ref> Dutton stated that he believes Welcome to Country ceremonies are overdone but that they are "the respectful thing to do" for some events.<ref name="Welcome"/>
*'''28 April''': Chalmers and Minister for Finance [[Katy Gallagher]] released the Labor party's pre-election costings while announcing that student visa fees would rise to pay for extra spending.<ref>{{cite news |work=SBS News |url=https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/labor-to-fund-760m-in-election-promises-by-raising-international-student-visa-fees/n8gsjqhcr |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250428060259/https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/labor-to-fund-760m-in-election-promises-by-raising-international-student-visa-fees/n8gsjqhcr |archive-date=28 April 2025 |access-date=28 April 2025 |date=28 April 2025 |first=Ewa |last=Staszewska |title=Labor to fund $760 million in election promises by raising international student visa fees}}</ref> This led to calls from [[Credit rating agency|credit ratings agency]] [[S&P Global Ratings|S&P]] warned that Australia's credit rating could be downgraded unless spending was restrained.<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-28/labor-demands-coalition-costings-claims-1b-in-deficit-reduction/105224580 |date=28 April 2025 |title=Labor demands Coalition costings and claims $1b in deficit reduction |first=Jacob |last=Greber |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250428061803/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-28/labor-demands-coalition-costings-claims-1b-in-deficit-reduction/105224580 |archive-date=28 April 2025 |access-date=28 April 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Dutton stated that he does not believe Anzac Day services should involve a Welcome to Country ceremony.<ref name="Welcome">{{cite news |work=ABC News |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-28/peter-dutton-questions-welcome-to-country-at-anzac-dawn-services/105224902 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250428061928/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-28/peter-dutton-questions-welcome-to-country-at-anzac-dawn-services/105224902 |url-status=live |archive-date=28 April 2025 |access-date=28 April 2025 |date=28 April 2025 |title=Peter Dutton suggests Anzac Day dawn services should not involve Welcomes to Country |first=Samantha |last=Dick}}</ref>
*'''29 April''': Labor announced a $2 billion fund to enable state governments to partially fund the construction of new homes.<ref>{{cite news |work=The Sydney Morning Herald |url-status=live |url-access=subscription |archive-date=29 April 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250429022819/https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/labor-s-latest-bid-to-fix-housing-crisis-2-billion-for-states-to-build-more-homes-20250428-p5luv9.html |url=https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/labor-s-latest-bid-to-fix-housing-crisis-2-billion-for-states-to-build-more-homes-20250428-p5luv9.html |access-date=29 April 2025 |date=29 April 2025 |title=Labor's latest bid to fix housing crisis: $2 billion for states to build more homes |first=David |last=Crowe}}</ref> A press conference held by Dutton was interrupted by anti-nuclear protestors in [[Sanctuary Point]].<ref>{{cite news |work=SBS News |access-date=29 April 2025 |date=29 April 2025 |archive-date=29 April 2025 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250429050315/https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/anti-nuclear-protesters-disrupt-peter-duttons-campaign-event-in-nsw/07vq3iepb |url=https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/anti-nuclear-protesters-disrupt-peter-duttons-campaign-event-in-nsw/07vq3iepb |title=Anti-nuclear protesters disrupt Peter Dutton's campaign event in NSW |first=Sara |last=Tomevska}}</ref> Another Trumpet of Patriots candidate, Mark Aldridge, who was contesting the [[division of Makin]] in [[South Australia]] resigns in protest over the party's use of text message communications to voters. Because nominations have already closed, Aldridge's name would still appear as a ToP candidate on ballot papers.<ref>{{cite news |work=[[Mumbrella]] |access-date=30 April 2025 |title=Trumpet of Patriots candidate quits over spam texts, 'false promises' |first=Lauren |last=McNamara |date=29 April 2025 |url=https://mumbrella.com.au/trumpet-of-patriots-candidate-quits-over-spam-texts-false-promises-872513 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250430021941/https://mumbrella.com.au/trumpet-of-patriots-candidate-quits-over-spam-texts-false-promises-872513 |url-status=live |archive-date=30 April 2025}}</ref>
*'''30 April''': Dutton accused Labor of trying to resurrect the failed [[Voice to Parliament]] proposal following Wong's statement that people in ten years would say "Did we even have an argument about that?", she later told SBS News that "the voice is gone", and Albanese also shot down Dutton's notion.<ref>{{cite news |work=[[National Indigenous Times]] |url=https://nit.com.au/30-04-2025/17667/pm-wong-deny-voice-is-being-revived |title=PM, Wong deny Voice will be revived |date=30 April 2025 |first=Dechlan |last=Brennan |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20250430133850/https://nit.com.au/30-04-2025/17667/pm-wong-deny-voice-is-being-revived |url-status=live |archive-date=30 April 2025 |access-date=30 April 2025}}</ref> The headline rate of [[inflation]] stayed stagnant at 2.4% and trimmed mean inflation fell to 2.9%. Chalmers welcomed this news.<ref>{{cite news |url-status=live |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-30/inflation-march-quarter-2025-stable/105232824 |first=Gareth |last=Hucthens |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250430052850/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-30/inflation-march-quarter-2025-stable/105232824 |archive-date=30 April 2025 |access-date=30 April 2025 |work=ABC News |title=Headline inflation stable at 2.4pc while RBA's preferred measure drops within target |date=30 April 2025}}</ref> The Liberal Party announced that they would take [[Boroondara City Council]] to court due to the council's intention to remove their candidate Amelia Hamer's signage.<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-30/kooyong-monique-ryan-amelia-hamer-sign-injunction/105233842 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250430104319/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-30/kooyong-monique-ryan-amelia-hamer-sign-injunction/105233842 |url-status=live |archive-date=30 April 2025 |access-date=30 April 2025 |date=30 April 2025 |title=Kooyong corflute kerfuffle headed for court as Liberals cry foul at council over Amelia Hamer sign removal |first=Tom |last=Crowley}}</ref> The Greens officially launched their campaign later in the day.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-30/federal-election-2025-live-albanese-dutton-campaign/105231912 |access-date=30 April 2025 |title=Federal election 2025: Final days see a campaigning blitz by major political parties — as it happened |at=Greens campaign launch gets underway in Melbourne |work=ABC News |date=30 April 2025 |first1=Georgie |last1=Hewson |last2=Gould |first2=Courtney |first3=Joshua |last3=Boscaini |name-list-style=and |archive-date=30 April 2025 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20250430141501/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-30/federal-election-2025-live-albanese-dutton-campaign/105231912}}</ref> Albanese delivered a speech and takes questions at the [[National Press Club (Australia)|National Press Club]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=2025-04-30 |title='Greatest honour of my life': PM's final press club address before federal election |url=https://www.9news.com.au/national/federal-election-2025-anthony-albaneses-final-national-press-club-address/60d55533-7973-483a-8433-5b41f6cf5a3f |first=Yashee |last=Sharma |url-status=live |archive-date=1 May 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250501084005/https://www.9news.com.au/national/federal-election-2025-anthony-albaneses-final-national-press-club-address/60d55533-7973-483a-8433-5b41f6cf5a3f |access-date=2025-05-01 |website=Nine News Australia}}</ref> Dutton announced a policy to fund infrastructure projects further in the [[Indo-Pacific region]].<ref>{{cite news |work=SBS News |url=https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/coalition-looking-to-spend-billions-in-this-battleground-for-influence/2nrmivmvt |archive-date=30 April 2025 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250430004345/https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/coalition-looking-to-spend-billions-in-this-battleground-for-influence/2nrmivmvt |title=Why the Coalition is promising to spend billions in this 'battleground for influence' |date=30 April 2025 |first=Cameron |last=Carr |access-date=1 May 2025}}</ref>
*'''1 May''': The Coalition released their policy costings, revealing they would institute a tax on [[vapes]] and assist the creation of a Jewish Arts Quarter in [[Melbourne]] to be funded by the cutting of funding to [[Creative Australia]].<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250501063716/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-05-01/coalition-costings-reveal-worse-budget-bottom-line-than-labor/105239712 |archive-date=1 May 2025 |date=1 May 2025 |url-status=live |title=Coalition election promise costings reveal worse budget bottom line for two years compared to Labor's |first=Tom |last=Crowley |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-05-01/coalition-costings-reveal-worse-budget-bottom-line-than-labor/105239712 |access-date=1 May 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |work=The Guardian |url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/may/02/coalition-arts-funding-election-costings-creative-australia-jewish-quarter-project-broadcasting-ntwnfb |access-date=2 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250502023652/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/may/02/coalition-arts-funding-election-costings-creative-australia-jewish-quarter-project-broadcasting-ntwnfb |archive-date=2 May 2025 |title=Coalition to cut 10% of Creative Australia funding to divert to Melbourne Jewish Arts Quarter |url-status=live |first=Kelly |last=Burke |date=2 May 2025}}</ref> The AEC announced that more than 4.8 million votes have already been cast at pre-poll centres across the country.<ref>{{Cite news |last2=Dhanji |first2=Krishani |first1=Caitlin |last1=Cassidy |date=2025-05-01 |title=Coalition plans to have two nuclear power plants operating by mid-2030s – as it happened |url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2025/may/01/australia-election-2025-live-anthony-albanese-peter-dutton-campaign-policies-economy-inflation-cost-of-living-coalition-labor-ntwnfb?page=with:block-6812cc778f0870089fc04c12&filterKeyEvents=false#liveblog-navigation |name-list-style=and |url-status=live |archive-date=1 May 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250501082507/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2025/may/01/australia-election-2025-live-anthony-albanese-peter-dutton-campaign-policies-economy-inflation-cost-of-living-coalition-labor-ntwnfb?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-6812c2128f083cd6dad7fcf8#block-6812c2128f083cd6dad7fcf8 |at=Nearly 5 million Australians have voted in pre-poll ahead of the 3 May election |access-date=2025-05-01 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref>
* '''3 May''': About 8.5 million early votes across all types (postal, in person, and mobile teams) were cast prior to election day.<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-05-03/federal-election-results-seats-to-watch-polls-close/100031090 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250504192955/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-05-03/federal-election-results-seats-to-watch-polls-close/100031090 |access-date=7 May 2025 |archive-date=4 May 2025 |title=Your federal election guide of seats to watch as vote counting begins and polls close |date=3 May 2025 |url-status=live |first=Casey |last=Briggs |at=Early voting has changed election night}}</ref>

