=== Preferences ===
As is tradition in Australian elections, several parties recommended voters' [[Preferential voting|preferences]] be directed to certain candidates. The Coalition recommended voters direct their second preference votes to the One Nation candidate or the Libertarian candidate. One Nation also chose to preference Coalition candidates second in some seats.<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-23/pauline-hanson-one-nation-preferencing-deal/105200748 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250425050605/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-23/pauline-hanson-one-nation-preferencing-deal/105200748 |archive-date=25 April 2025 |access-date=25 April 2025 |url-status=live |date=23 April 2025 |first=Annabel |last=Crabb |author-link=Annabel Crabb |title=Coalition cosies up to One Nation with preferences in ceasefire after 30-year war}}</ref> In electorates where the Liberal and National parties are running against each other, the National candidate in these areas recommended preferences be directed to the Liberal candidate second, then preferences recommended to One Nation, with Labor and the Greens candidates usually last. [[Teal independent]] candidates supported by [[Climate 200]] generally did not recommend preferences.<ref>{{cite news |work=ABC News |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250501073408/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-05-01/how-to-vote-cards-in-your-electorates/105217758?utm_id=2547236&sfmc_id=416517318 |url-status=live |archive-date=1 May 2025 |access-date=1 May 2025 |date=1 May 2025 |first1=Matt |last1=Martino |last2=Shatoba |first2=Katia |first3=Thomas |last3=Brettell |first4=Alex |last4=Palmer |first5=Mark |last5=Doman |name-list-style=and |title=How major parties want you to number the ballot in your electorate |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-05-01/how-to-vote-cards-in-your-electorates/105217758?utm_id=2547236&sfmc_id=416517318}}</ref>

The Labor Party chose to preference the Greens second in most seats except for [[Division of Macnamara|Macnamara]] due to the Greens' pro-Palestinian leanings and the seat having a large [[Jews in Australia|Jewish]] population.<ref name="Greens">{{cite news |work=ABC News |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-24/how-preference-deals-could-change-who-wins-in-some-seats/105206966 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250424040604/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-24/how-preference-deals-could-change-who-wins-in-some-seats/105206966 |archive-date=24 April 2025 |url-status=live |first1=Alison |last1=Xiao |last2=Tregenza |first2=Holly |name-list-style=and |date=24 April 2025 |access-date=25 April 2025 |title=How preference deals could change who wins in some seats}}</ref> The Greens directed voters to preference [[Australia's Voice]], pro-Palestinian candidates, and teal independents higher than Labor. The Greens also chose to preference Labor higher than the Coalition in every seat that they contested.<ref>{{cite news |work=The Sydney Morning Herald |url=https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/bandt-retaliates-against-labor-by-putting-teals-payman-before-government-20250422-p5ltez.html |access-date=25 April 2025 |archive-date=22 April 2025 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250422195750/https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/bandt-retaliates-against-labor-by-putting-teals-payman-before-government-20250422-p5ltez.html |title=Bandt retaliates against Labor by putting teals, Payman before government |first=Paul |last=Sakkal |date=23 April 2025}}</ref><ref name="Greens"/> Trumpet of Patriots chose to recommend voters preference incumbent candidates last, although in [[Division of Bennelong|Bennelong]] they recommended preferencing the incumbent Labor candidate [[Jerome Laxale]] above the Liberal candidate Scott Yung but preferenced incumbent teal independents below the Liberal candidate.<ref>{{cite news |work=The Sydney Morning Herald |access-date=30 April 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250418071356/https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/teal-independents-to-get-an-unlikely-preference-boost-from-clive-palmer-20250418-p5lssq.html |url=https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/teal-independents-to-get-an-unlikely-preference-boost-from-clive-palmer-20250418-p5lssq.html |url-status=live |archive-date=18 April 2025 |title=Teal independents to get an unlikely preference boost – from Clive Palmer |first1=Natassia |last1=Chrysanthos |last2=Sakkal |first2=Paul |date=18 April 2025 |name-list-style=and}}</ref>

