== Aftermath and reactions ==
=== Domestic reactions ===
{{see also|2025 Liberal Party of Australia leadership election|2025 National Party of Australia leadership spill|2025 Australian Greens leadership election}}
[[File:People lining up to vote for the 2025 federal election at Merri-bek Primary School.jpg|thumb|A polling place in the [[division of Wills]] with material for Labor's [[Peter Khalil]] and the Greens' [[Samantha Ratnam]]]]
[[File:Australians voting in Shanghai - 000186991.jpg|thumb|Australians voting in the federal election in [[Shanghai]]]]
Prominent commentator and ABC chief election analyst [[Antony Green]] reported a second term for Labor within two and a half hours of east-coast polls closing. This was about 30 minutes faster than his projection of Labor ousting the Coalition at the prior federal election, but an hour later than his projection of the Coalition win at the federal election before that. This was followed by major media organisations also projecting a Labor win, as well as Peter Dutton's own seat loss.<ref name="Maguire 2025">{{cite news |last=Maguire |first=Danielle |date=4 May 2025 |title=Who won the 2025 Australian federal election? What happened to Peter Dutton? |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-05-04/who-won-the-2025-federal-election-who-is-prime-minister-/105248022 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250504013302/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-05-04/who-won-the-2025-federal-election-who-is-prime-minister-/105248022 |archive-date=4 May 2025 |access-date=4 May 2025 |work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]}}</ref><ref name="Reuters Dutton"/> Preliminary results<!-- 8:00{{Nbsp}}pm AEST on 4 May 2025 --> indicated that Labor had gained seats in all six states. Labor had taken at least 13 seats off the Coalition, including four in [[South East Queensland]], three in [[Sydney]], and both seats in Northern [[Tasmania]]. Labor was also projected to have won two inner [[Brisbane]] seats held by the Greens. The final result remained in doubt for 16 seats, half of which were in [[Victoria (state)|Victoria]].<ref name="Green 2025">{{cite web|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/elections/federal/2025/results?sortBy=latest&filter=all&selectedRegion=all&selectedParty=all&partyWonBy=all&partyHeldBy=all|title=Australian Federal Election 2025 Live Results|date=14 May 2025|access-date=14 May 2025|first=Antony|last=Green|work=ABC News|orig-date=3 May 2025}}</ref>

Dutton conceded defeat shortly after 9:30&nbsp;pm AEST on election night, announcing that he had called Anthony Albanese to congratulate him on Labor's re-election. In his concession speech, Dutton acknowledged the Coalition's poor performance and took full responsibility for the result. He also became the first sitting federal Opposition Leader to lose his own seat (won by [[Ali France]] of Labor) in a federal election.<ref name="Messenger 2025">{{cite news |last=Messenger |first=Andrew |date=3 May 2025 |title=Who is Ali France, the Labor candidate who has unseated Peter Dutton in Dickson? |url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/may/03/who-is-ali-france-labor-candidate-peter-dutton-seat-dickson-election-results |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250503153334/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/may/03/who-is-ali-france-labor-candidate-peter-dutton-seat-dickson-election-results |archive-date=3 May 2025 |access-date=3 May 2025 |work=The Guardian |issn=0261-3077}}</ref><ref name="Reuters Dutton">{{cite web|date=3 May 2025|url=https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/australia-state-broadcaster-abc-projects-opposition-leader-dutton-lose-seat-2025-05-03/|url-access=subscription|location=Sydney|editor-first=Praveen|editor-last=Menon|archive-date=3 May 2025|url-status=live|archive-url=https://archive.today/20250503130855/https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/australia-state-broadcaster-abc-projects-opposition-leader-dutton-lose-seat-2025-05-03/|title=Australia state broadcaster ABC projects opposition leader Dutton to lose seat|last=Pal|first=Alasdair|website=Reuters|access-date=3 May 2025}}</ref><ref name="Landslide">{{cite news |title=Australian election 2025: Labor secures historic and emphatic victory |url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2025/may/03/australian-federal-election-2025-live-news-today-anthony-albanese-labor-peter-dutton-liberal-coalition-greens-polls-vote-odds-results-politics-latest-updates |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250504014627/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2025/may/03/australian-federal-election-2025-live-news-today-anthony-albanese-labor-peter-dutton-liberal-coalition-greens-polls-vote-odds-results-politics-latest-updates |archive-date=4 May 2025 |first1=Krishani |last1=Dhanji |last2=Cassidy |first2=Caitlin |first3=Rafqa |last3=Touma |last4=Doherty |first4=Ben |name-list-style=and |work=The Guardian |date=4 May 2025 |access-date=4 May 2025}}</ref> Claiming victory on election night, Albanese addressed supporters with a message of unity and optimism, declaring it "a time of profound opportunity for our nation".<ref name="Landslide"/> He emphasised the importance of collective effort in shaping the country's future, stating, "We have everything we need to seize this moment and make it our own, but we must do it together."<ref name="Landslide"/>

Albanese's win has been described as a [[landslide victory]] and historic comeback,<ref name="BBC live results">{{cite news|date=2 May 2025|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/live/cevdw14r1mgt|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250503112907/https://www.bbc.com/news/live/cevdw14r1mgt|archive-date=3 May 2025|url-status=live|title=Australian opposition party reeling after Albanese's landslide election win|website=BBC News|access-date=3 May 2025|editor-first=Gavin|editor-last=Butler}}</ref> having been returned with an increased majority,<ref name="The Telegraph 2025">{{cite news |date=5 May 2025 |title=The Trump effect has placed both Canada and Australia under left-wing rule |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/opinion/2025/05/05/trump-effect-canada-australia-elections-left-wing/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250505141838/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/opinion/2025/05/05/trump-effect-canada-australia-elections-left-wing/ |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |archive-date=5 May 2025 |access-date=6 May 2025 |work=[[The Telegraph (London)|The Telegraph]] |author=Telegraph View |issn=0307-1235}}</ref> as well as exceeding most of the [[opinion polling]] showing either a smaller majority for the Labor government or a [[hung parliament]].<ref name="The Guardian live">{{cite news |title=Labor on track for huge majority after landslide win – as it happened |url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2025/may/03/australian-federal-election-2025-live-news-today-anthony-albanese-labor-peter-dutton-liberal-coalition-greens-polls-vote-odds-results-politics-latest-updates |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250504014627/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2025/may/03/australian-federal-election-2025-live-news-today-anthony-albanese-labor-peter-dutton-liberal-coalition-greens-polls-vote-odds-results-politics-latest-updates |archive-date=4 May 2025 |first1=Krishani |last1=Dhanji |last2=Cassidy |first2=Caitlin |first3=Rafqa |last3=Touma |last4=Doherty |first4=Ben |name-list-style=and |work=The Guardian |date=3 May 2025 |access-date=4 May 2025}}</ref><ref name="Worthington 2025">{{cite news |title=Anthony Albanese and Labor claim landslide election win as Peter Dutton loses seat |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250503152651/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-05-03/federal-election-anthony-albanese-wins/105247712 |url-status=live|archive-date=3 May 2025 |first=Brett |last=Worthington |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-05-03/federal-election-anthony-albanese-wins/105247712 |work=ABC News |date=3 May 2025 |access-date=4 May 2025}}</ref><ref name="Olbrycht-Palmer & Williamson 2025">{{cite news |title=Federal Election 2025: Winners and Losers after Labor's landslide |url=https://www.news.com.au/national/federal-election/voting-and-results/federal-election-2025-winners-and-losers-after-labors-landslide/news-story/c21e2d8db0e7567ac117d9d57f91f805 |archive-date=4 May 2025 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20250504045348/https://www.news.com.au/national/federal-election/voting-and-results/federal-election-2025-winners-and-losers-after-labors-landslide/news-story/c21e2d8db0e7567ac117d9d57f91f805 |first1=Joseph |last1=Olbrycht-Palmer |first2=Joanne |last2=Williamson |work=NewsWire |via=news.com.au |date=4 May 2025 |access-date=4 May 2025}}</ref> As a result, comparisons have been made to the [[2025 Canadian federal election|Canadian federal election]] held earlier in the same week where the incumbent [[centre-left]] minority government,<ref name="The Telegraph 2025"/> led by the [[Liberal Party of Canada|Liberal Party]], overturned a significant deficit in polling, made gains, and won another term. Commentators spoke of a negative "Trump effect" as [[Conservative Party of Canada|Conservative]] opposition leader [[Pierre Poilievre]] also lost his seat.<ref>{{cite web |last=Smith |first=David |date=4 May 2025 |title=Blaming Donald Trump for conservative losses in both Canada and Australia is being too kind to Peter Dutton |url-status=live |archive-date=6 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250506013518/https://theconversation.com/blaming-donald-trump-for-conservative-losses-in-both-canada-and-australia-is-being-too-kind-to-peter-dutton-255599 |url=https://theconversation.com/blaming-donald-trump-for-conservative-losses-in-both-canada-and-australia-is-being-too-kind-to-peter-dutton-255599 |access-date=5 May 2025 |website=The Conversation}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Duncan |first=Grant |date=5 May 2025 |title=A 'Trump slump' has lifted the left in Canada and now Australia – what are the lessons for NZ? |url=https://theconversation.com/a-trump-slump-has-lifted-the-left-in-canada-and-now-australia-what-are-the-lessons-for-nz-255715 |url-status=live |archive-date=5 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250505042751/https://theconversation.com/a-trump-slump-has-lifted-the-left-in-canada-and-now-australia-what-are-the-lessons-for-nz-255715 |access-date=5 May 2025 |website=The Conversation}}</ref> According to the BBC's Australia correspondent Katy Watson, United States president [[Donald Trump]] was "the gamechanger" and Albanese was able "to convince voters he was a safer pair of hands in an uncertain world".<ref name="BBC live results"/><ref>{{cite web |url-status=live |archive-date=4 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250504200451/https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cdxgwnj8v5eo |last1=Watson |first1=Katy |last2=Ng |first2=Kelly |location=Sydney and Brisbane |date=3 May 2025 |title=He wanted to be Australia's PM. But a 'Trump effect' thwarted Peter Dutton |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cdxgwnj8v5eo |access-date=5 May 2025 |website=BBC News}}</ref>

The election marked the first time since [[2007 Australian federal election|2007]], and the third overall since [[Federation of Australia|Federation]], that a leader of either of the major parties lost their seat in Parliament.<ref>{{cite web |last=Green |first=Antony |title=The Gurgle Hole of History – Leaders who've lost their Seats at Elections |url=https://antonygreen.com.au/the-gurgle-hole-of-history-leaders-whove-lost-their-seats-at-elections/ |url-status=live |archive-date=6 February 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250206165943/https://antonygreen.com.au/the-gurgle-hole-of-history-leaders-whove-lost-their-seats-at-elections/ |date=20 February 2021 |website=Antony Green's Election Blog |access-date=4 May 2025}}</ref> Additionally, Albanese became the first [[Prime Minister of Australia|prime minister]] to be re-elected after serving a full term since [[John Howard]] won his fourth and last term in [[2004 Australian federal election|2004]],<ref>{{cite web |last=Daniel |first=Orestes Georgiou |date=3 May 2025 |title=Australian PM Anthony Albanese re-elected for a second three-year term |url=https://www.euronews.com/2025/05/03/early-vote-count-shows-australias-labour-party-set-to-win-second-term |access-date=5 May 2025 |url-status=live |archive-date=4 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250504025334/https://www.euronews.com/2025/05/03/early-vote-count-shows-australias-labour-party-set-to-win-second-term |website=[[Euronews]]}}</ref> and the first Labor leader to do so since [[Bob Hawke]]'s re-election in [[1990 Australian federal election|1990]].<ref name="BBC live results" /> It is the first time a Labor government has been re-elected with a majority since [[1993 Australian federal election|1993]], and the largest number of seats ever for Labor. This was the fourth consecutive election since [[2013 Australian federal election|2013]] that the Coalition lost primary vote support. The Liberals were reduced to their smallest presence in the House since their founding in 1945, and turned in the worst election result for the main non-Labor party since the [[United Australia Party]] (immediate forerunner of the Liberals) was reduced to 14 seats in [[1943 Australian federal election|1943]]. The significant swing against the Liberal Party was called "diabolical" by former senior Liberal minister [[Simon Birmingham]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Yussuf |first1=Ahmed |first2=Veronica |last2=Apap |first3=Georgie |last3=Hewson |last4=Wiseman |first4=Lewis |first5=Luke |last5=Cooper |last6=Thorpe |first6=Andrew |first7=Caitlin |last7=Rawling |first8=Maani |last8=Truu |last9=Tregenza |first9=Holly |first10=Joshua |last10=Boscaini |last11=Gould |first11=Courtney |name-list-style=and |date=3 May 2025 |title=Federal Election 2025: Anthony Albanese claims victory for Labor as Peter Dutton voted out of seat of Dickson — as it happened |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-05-03/federal-election-2025-live-anthony-albanese-peter-dutton/105245936?utm_campaign=abc_news_web&utm_content=link&utm_medium=content_shared&utm_source=abc_news_web#live-blog-post-176566 |access-date=3 May 2025 |work=ABC News}}</ref>

A particularly notable outcome was the dramatic decline of the [[Australian Greens]] in the lower house. The party lost three of its four seats, including the long-held seat of [[Division of Melbourne|Melbourne]], where Greens leader [[Adam Bandt]] was defeated by Labor's [[Sarah Witty]], ending his 15-year tenure in Parliament. The Greens' only remaining seat in the House of Representatives was [[Division of Ryan|Ryan]] in Queensland, held by [[Elizabeth Watson-Brown]]. Analysts attributed the Greens' losses to a combination of factors, including controversial stances on foreign policy, particularly regarding the [[Israel-Gaza War|Israel-Gaza conflict]], and a perceived shift away from core environmental issues, leading to voter alienation in key electorates.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2025-05-08 |title=Greens leader Adam Bandt concedes seat, ending 15 years in parliament |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-05-08/adam-bandt-concedes-seat-of-melbourne/105268636 |first=Maani |last=Truu |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250509205320/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-05-08/adam-bandt-concedes-seat-of-melbourne/105268636 |archive-date=9 May 2025 |access-date=2025-05-13 |work=ABC News |language=en-AU}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=2025-05-08 |title=Adam Bandt has lost his seat of Melbourne. What happened to the Greens in Victoria? |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-05-08/federal-election-result-adam-bandt-greens-melbourne-vote-2025/105266174 |first1=Kate |last1=Ashton |first2=Madi |last2=Chwasta |name-list-style=and |access-date=2025-05-13 |work=ABC News |language=en-AU |archive-date=11 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250511161416/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-05-08/federal-election-result-adam-bandt-greens-melbourne-vote-2025/105266174 |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[Clive Palmer]] spent approximately $60 million in the 2025 federal election on a nationwide advertising blitz for [[Trumpet of Patriots]], including over $6 million on [[YouTube]] and [[Meta Platforms|Meta]] platforms, and sending over 17 million text messages; the party failed to secure any seats in Parliament, garnering about 1.85% of the national vote. Following this defeat, Palmer announced his retirement from politics, citing his age and a desire to focus on philanthropic efforts.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-05-05 |title=Clive Palmer spent about $245 per first preference vote in Trumpet of Patriot's failed election run |url=https://www.news.com.au/national/politics/clive-palmer-spent-about-245-per-first-preference-vote-in-trumpet-of-patriots-failed-election-run/news-story/6c822ee9b9bb4886889304ea025d4345 |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20250514050654/https://www.news.com.au/national/politics/clive-palmer-spent-about-245-per-first-preference-vote-in-trumpet-of-patriots-failed-election-run/news-story/6c822ee9b9bb4886889304ea025d4345 |url-status=live |archive-date=14 May 2025 |first=Jessica |last=Wang |access-date=2025-05-14 |via=''news.com.au'' |work=NewsWire}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |website=[[Forbes Australia]] |url=https://www.forbes.com.au/news/leadership/clive-palmers-60-million-election-campaign-ends-in-failure/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250514024609/https://www.forbes.com.au/news/leadership/clive-palmers-60-million-election-campaign-ends-in-failure/ |url-status=live |archive-date=14 May 2025 |access-date=14 May 2025 |title=How billionaire Clive Palmer burned $60 million on a party that won nothing |first=Samuel |last=Hussey |date=5 May 2025}}</ref>

On 14 May, it was reported that a worker for the [[Australian Electoral Commission]] had not returned a container containing 1,866 ballots for the [[division of Barton]] from their home. The votes had already been counted and so the electoral result was not affected.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2025-05-14 |first=Pat |last=McGrath |at=Exclusive |title=AEC investigates after missing ballot papers found at election worker's home |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-05-14/aec-investigating-after-ballot-papers-missing-barton-sydney/105286812 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250513225937/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-05-14/aec-investigating-after-ballot-papers-missing-barton-sydney/105286812 |url-status=live |archive-date=13 May 2025 |access-date=2025-05-14 |work=ABC News |language=en-AU}}</ref>

=== International reactions ===
* {{flag|Canada}}: Prime Minister [[Mark Carney]] congratulated Albanese on social media, and stated: "In an increasingly divided world, Canada and Australia are close partners and the most reliable of friends."<ref>{{cite tweet |number=1918698545982357971 |user=MarkJCarney |title=Congratulations, @AlboMP, on your re-election today and the continued trust placed in you by Australians. |quote=In an increasingly divided world, Canada and Australia are close partners and the most reliable of friends. We have the opportunity to build on that relationship and our shared values to the benefit of both our nations, and I look forward to it. |first=Mark |last=Carney |date=3 May 2025 |access-date=3 May 2025 |url-status=live |archive-date=4 May 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250504054930/https://x.com/MarkJCarney/status/1918698545982357971}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.cbc.ca/news/world/australian-election-2025-1.7525798|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250503132009/https://www.cbc.ca/news/world/australian-election-2025-1.7525798|url-status=live|archive-date=3 May 2025|title=Anthony Albanese, riding anti-Trump wave, claims victory for Labor Party in Australia|website=[[CBC News]] |agency=The Associated Press and [[Thomson Reuters]]|date=3 May 2025|access-date=3 May 2025}}</ref>
* {{flag|China}}: The [[Ministry of Foreign Affairs (China)|Ministry of Foreign Affairs]] congratulated Albanese and stated that China "stands ready to work with the new Australian government led by Prime Minister Albanese" to advance "a more mature, stable, and productive comprehensive strategic partnership".<ref>{{cite web |date=4 May 2025 |title=China congratulates Australia's Labor Party on federal election win |url-status=live |first=Victoria |last=Bela |archive-date=5 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250505100949/https://www.scmp.com/news/china/diplomacy/article/3309018/china-congratulates-australias-labor-party-federal-election-win |url=https://www.scmp.com/news/china/diplomacy/article/3309018/china-congratulates-australias-labor-party-federal-election-win |access-date=5 May 2025 |website=[[South China Morning Post]] }}</ref>
* {{flag|Estonia}}: Prime Minister [[Kristen Michal]] congratulated Albanese on his re-election and emphasised the two countries' long-distance relationship.<ref>{{cite tweet |number=1918918120825114860 |user=KristenMichalPM |title=Congratulations on your election victory, @AlboMP. |quote=Despite the distance between us, Estonia and Australia stand united as like-minded partners, committed to democracy and unwavering support to Ukraine. Looking forward to deepening our cooperation in the years ahead. 🇪🇪🤝🇦🇺
|first=Kristen |last=Michal |date=3 May 2025 |access-date=3 May 2025 |url-status=live |archive-date=4 May 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250504223518/https://x.com/KristenMichalPM/status/1918918120825114860}}</ref>
* {{flag|Fiji}}: Prime Minister [[Sitiveni Rabuka]] congratulated Albanese on his re-election, and said he is looking forward to continuing their partnership, grounded in mutual respect, development cooperation, and regional stability.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Rabuka congratulates Albanese on re-election as Australian PM|url=https://www.fijivillage.com/news/Rabuka-congratulates-Albanese-on-re-election-as-Australian-PM-548rfx/|website=www.fijivillage.com|access-date=2025-05-14|language=en|last=Narawa|date=4 May 2025|first=Alipate}}</ref>
* {{flag|France}}: President [[Emmanuel Macron]] congratulated Albanese on his re-election, and said: "In the face of global challenges, Australia and France have so much to achieve together — especially in the Indo-Pacific. Let us continue to write, with ambition and friendship, the new chapter of our partnership."<ref>{{cite tweet |number=1918668865191530723 |user=EmmanuelMacron |title=Congratulations, dear Prime Minister @AlboMP, on your re-election. |quote=In the face of global challenges, Australia and France have so much to achieve together — especially in the Indo-Pacific. Let us continue to write, with ambition and friendship, the new chapter of our partnership. |first=Emmanuel |last=Macron |date=3 May 2025 |access-date=3 May 2025 |url-status=live |archive-date=4 May 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250504055359/https://x.com/EmmanuelMacron/status/1918668865191530723}}</ref>
* {{flag|Germany}}: Chancellor [[Olaf Scholz]] congratulated Albanese and his party on the election victory.<ref>{{cite tweet |number=1918672669504847975 |url-status=live |archive-date=14 May 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250514020300/https://x.com/Bundeskanzler/status/1918672669504847975 |user=Bundeskanzler |title=Many congratulations to Australian Prime Minister @AlboMP and his party on the election victory |quote= Germany and Australia's cooperation is close in trade and business, science as well as in security policy issues. This is good for both our nations. |first=Olaf |last=Scholz |date=3 May 2025 |access-date=7 May 2025}}</ref>
